\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  action seen if dollar/yen hits near 80\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2009\par 23:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * No immediate risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MoF may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if dollar/yen is above 85\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to buy U.S. Treasuries\par \par \uc2 * Still no substitute for the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, July 16 (Reuters) - The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the U.S. dollar trades close to 80 yen, a senior economist at Mizuho Securities told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the rate is over 85 yen," said the Tokyo-based Naoki Iizuka, senior economist at Mizuho Securities. "If dollar/yen falls close to 80 yen, then I think the MoF will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Maybe the area of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be between 80-85 yen."\par \par \uc2 Speculation about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  resurfaced in recent sessions after the dollar fell to 91.73 yen on Monday on electronic trading platform EBS <JPY=EBS>, a five-month low. Last week, dollar/yen traded to a low at 91.80.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had fallen to a 13-year trough around 87.10 yen in January as jittery investors flocked to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a shelter from the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 That raised concerns over the impact of a strong yen on an export-reliant economy like Japan as it emerges from its deepest recession since World War Two.\par \par \uc2 Japan has a history of trying to slow the yen's strength by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars, but it has stayed out of the market since a 35 trillion yen campaign over 15-month span ended in March 2004. The MoF has gradually moved away from heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because that last round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had mixed results, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 But there had been "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " every now and then over the last few years where a Japanese MoF official would express concern about "volatility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would say that the government is closely monitoring the market.\par \par \uc2 Late on Thursday, the the dollar was down 0.4 percent at 93.9 yen.\par \par \uc2 Iizuka thinks dollar/yen at 100 yen is an "equilibrium rate", with the pair likely to trade within 10 percent of that level or anywhere between 90-110 yen.\par \par \uc2 The Mizuho economist believes the 100-yen rate would be achieved by late 2009 or in the first half of 2010. "There is a great risk of the yen appreciating in 2010."\par \par \uc2 He thinks the Obama administration may need to add further stimulus measures to boost the U.S. economy by the second half of 2009 or 2010, but would be constrained to do so because of the negative impact on the already huge U.S. fiscal deficit.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. would then need to increase its exports and so there would be an incentive to weaken the dollar. I think dollar/yen will probably trade around 90 yen," Iizuka said.\par \par \uc2 "But the level is not that important. What is important is a gradual appreciation of the yen and its rise will be managed."\par \par \uc2 On U.S. Treasuries, Iizuka said the Japanese government has "no choice but to continue buying" these securities.\par \par \uc2 "If there is a serious decline in the U.S. dollar in the near future, not only Japan, but also China will lose their assets," said Iizuka said. "A dollar collapse would be an economic Armageddon."\par \par \uc2 Right now, he stressed that there is no substitute to the U.S. dollar as the world's top reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN/YEN|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|J|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090716e57g001vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
