\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A - Will  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Masayuki Kitano and Charlotte Cooper   \par \uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2009\par 09:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 9 (Reuters) - The yen has torn to its highest level against the dollar in five months and within sight of a 13-year high set in January, prompting speculation that Japan might try to curb its rise to prevent it damaging exports.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit 91.80 per dollar on trading platform EBS on Wednesday, leaving the dollar down 2 percent on the day. The dollar has firmed on Thursday to 93.30 yen but still stands 8 percent below a six-month high traded in April.\par \par \uc2 The yen also surged to its highest in two months against the euro, the Australian dollar and sterling. All three have recovered 2-3 yen on Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily earlier in the decade to stop a rising yen from harming its all-important exports.\par \par \uc2 But they haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, the end of a 15-month long spree in which they sold 35 trillion yen ($375 billion) to protect growth.\par \par \uc2 WOULD THEY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2  NOW?\par \par \uc2 Unlikely. The yen may have been on a tear in the last 24 hours, but it surged 27 percent against the dollar from a low in August to a high in January. If authorities didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  then, they are unlikely to do so now.\par \par \uc2 Also, authorities may have felt more temptation to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last year because exports were collapsing as the crisis weighed on global demand. Exports are still showing significant falls from a year earlier, but monthly changes suggest the worst of the slump is over.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a 13-year low of 87.10 yen in January as the global economic crisis saw panicked investors repatriating funds, forcing liquidation of deeply embedded short-yen positions which had built up over years.\par \par \uc2 Even a green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Group of Seven industrial powers last October did not persuade Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 At that time, the G7 issued a rare inter-meeting statement singling out yen volatility, which was interpreted as giving Japan the approval of its fellow big nations to stem the yen surge if it wanted to.\par \par \uc2 "Even when the dollar fell to 87 yen, there was not any sign that there might be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so if you ask whether the probability will increase because of the fall to 92 yen from 95 yen, that does not seem to be the case," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for JPMorgan Chase Bank in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "There has not been any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2004. The policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has probably changed."\par \par \uc2 WHAT DO THE OFFICIALS SAY?\par \par \uc2 The top government spokesman on Thursday repeated an often-used phrase that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were undesirable for the stability of the economy. [ID:nTKF106460]\par \par \uc2 When they are particularly concerned, they tend to step up verbal warnings before any actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , usually by saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take decisive actions in markets, or something to that effect.\par \par \uc2 On May 22, when the yen was trading around 94 per dollar, Finance Minister Kaoru Yosano said he was not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nTFD003303]\par \par \uc2 SO IS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  OUT OF THE QUESTION?\par \par \uc2 The possibility increases below 90 yen per dollar and the risk gets much higher at or below 87. But just a drop in the dollar is not necessarily enough to spark \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to watch the yen's exchange rate against other trading partners as well and keep its eye on the Nikkei share average as a barometer of the impact of a stronger yen on Japanese companies.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei hit a 26-year closing low of 7,054.98 in March but closed on Thursday at 9,291.06, down 1.4 percent on the day but 32 percent above the March level.\par \par \uc2 "The simultaneous move is important -- dollar/yen below 90, the Nikkei below 8,000, and the yen crosses all down are the necessary conditions (for verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," said Masafumi Yamamoto, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Even if the dollar/yen falls below 90 and the Nikkei is above 9,000, there's not the case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 BUT THE SWISS NATIONAL BANK HAS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENED\b0 \highlight\uc2 , WHY NOT \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 As the world's second-largest economy, Japan would cause bigger trade tensions by trying to hold down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Switzerland, the world's 21st-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has raised its assessment of the economy. While still deep in recession, economic conditions are not getting worse.\par \par \uc2 Tanase notes the yen's real effective exchange rate is around its long-term average, meaning it is not particularly strong at the moment.\par \par \uc2 "To stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the yen's level is not strong would lead to maintaining (trade) competitiveness," Tanase said\par \par \uc2 "The chances are probably low that such action by Japan would be tolerated at a time when economic conditions in other countries are also very poor." ($1=93.19 Yen) (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090709e579000fa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
