\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  one step closer to  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  as yen surges\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2009\par 23:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Sharp move in yen increases threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Yen may keep rising as risk aversion increases\par \par \uc2 By Vivianne Rodrigues and Nick Olivari\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, July 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be one step closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in five years as a soaring yen further jeopardizes the country's chances of pulling out of recession.\par \par \uc2 Growing unease about the global economy has prompted investors to rush out of trades that bet against the yen while favoring higher-yielding but often riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move accelerated on Wednesday and spurred talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or at least jaw-boning of the exchange rate by Japanese officials, after investors bought back yen, pushing it to multi-month highs against both the dollar and euro.\par \par \uc2 If the yen strengthens to trade below 90 to the dollar, "it is more a question of 'when' than 'if'," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon, in New York. "At this stage, the pace of the move rather than the absolute, is their main concern. They want to see stability, they have mentioned it many times before."\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have said repeatedly the country is not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. However, given the country's reliance on exports, they may be more motivated.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also mindful that the Group of Seven industrial powers in October issued a rare inter-meeting statement singling out yen volatility, giving Japanese authorities the green light to stem its surge at that time.\par \par \uc2 The yen also jumped against the Australian and the New Zealand dollars as it quickly broke through key levels around 93.40 to the U.S. dollar to touch its highest since February.\par \par \uc2 The yen moved more than 3 percent against both the dollar <JPY=> and euro <EURJPY=> Wednesday in its biggest one-day moves since March and November, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Japan has a long history of trying to stem yen strength by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars, but it has stayed out of the market since a 35 trillion yen ($377 billion) campaign over 15 months ended in March 2004. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gradually moved away from heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because that last campaign had mixed results.\par \par \uc2 A summit of the Group of Eight major economies in Italy starting on Wednesday was silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the sharp moves are unlikely to go unnoticed, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "As the yen starts getting close to the 90 mark, we are going to see Japan stepping up the rhetoric at least," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Wells Fargo Bank in New York. "It's inevitable."\par \par \uc2 Central banks have become more active in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent months to prevent price volatility from threatening a nascent economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 FOLLOWING THE SWISS EXAMPLE?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  surprised the market in March by buying euros and U.S. dollars <CHF=> and selling the franc. It was the first time that the SNB had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1995.\par \par \uc2 The SNB denied they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- instead acting to stop it from rising. Swiss monetary authorities have sold the franc several times after the March announcement, with more aggressive forays in the last days of June, traders said. As a result, the Swiss franc has fallen about 4 percent versus the euro <EURCHF=> since March.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also strengthened in the past as investors unwound carry trades, trading around 88 to the dollar late last year as the global economic crisis intensified.\par \par \uc2 Momentum is now building again to hold yen as risk aversion rises.\par \par \uc2 "As risk aversion goes up, the yen is regaining some of the safe-haven characteristics it has lost," said Serebriakov. "Carry trade positions now are not as large as they were pre-crisis, but the yen will keep benefiting. And let's see what happens if we get to the 90 mark. That's probably the line in the sand." (Additional reporting by Wanfeng Zhou; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF|ABX|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090708e578001jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
