\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Major nations should back dlr as key  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 -Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2009\par 10:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the G8 summit click) * Japan backs dollar to remain as global key reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  * Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate unlikely at G8 plus G5 * Japan pushes global agriculture investment principles\par \par \uc2 (adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 3 (Reuters) - Major countries should support the dollar as the key international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although emerging nations may discuss a new global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the sidelines of the G8 summit next week, a Japanese official said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 China has asked for debate on a new global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when leaders from the Group of Eight (G8) meet with the G5 emerging economies next week in Italy, G8 sources told Reuters. News of the Chinese request pushed the dollar down to a three-week low on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But Japan thinks it would be difficult for another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to replace the dollar as the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it is against any move that would unnecessarily weaken the status of the dollar, said Yoichi Suzuki, director-general of the Japanese foreign ministry's economic affairs bureau.\par \par \uc2 "Japan's stance is that major countries should support the dollar," Suzuki, one of the country's main coordinators for the G8 summit, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "It won't benefit any country to talk about ideas for a new global key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would weaken the dollar," said Suzuki.\par \par \uc2 An idea, which China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has floated, that the International Monetary Fund's Special Drawing Right (SDR) could eventually displace the dollar as the principal reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unrealistic, he added.\par \par \uc2 Japan is interested in a stable dollar for its trade with other countries and because most of its $1 trillion foreign reserves, the world's second largest, are in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 Suzuki said he would not be surprised if the G5 discussed the role of the dollar when they meet on the sidelines of the G8 summit in L'Aquila.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister He Yafei said it would be "normal" if diversification of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system were discussed at next week's summit.\par \par \uc2 But Suzuki played down the idea, saying Tokyo does not expect the topic to be discussed at the meeting of G8 plus G5 emerging nations, including China, Brazil and India.\par \par \uc2 "LAND GRAB"\par \par \uc2 Talks at the July 8-10 summit are expected to focus on the state of the world economy, financial regulation, climate change and trade and development.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo will propose establishing principles and citing best practice to promote responsible investment in agriculture to cope with farmland acquisition or "land grabs" in developing countries, Suzuki said.\par \par \uc2 "Agriculture investment in developing countries should not be done because of trying to avoid tough environmental standards in industrial nations," Suzuki said, noting that Japan has been getting support for this initiative from G8 counterparts.\par \par \uc2 By taking the lead on this proposal, Japan -- the world's largest net food importer -- also hopes to gain support from developing countries that may have been hurt by irresponsible investment by foreign countries and companies, for Japanese firms' overseas investment in agriculture, he said.\par \par \uc2 On a call by French President Nicolas Sarkozy for the G8 to discuss regulating oil prices to prevent the wild swings of recent years, Suzuki said G8 officials have not been discussing it for the summit's agenda.\par \par \uc2 "Realistically, it would be hard to maintain oil prices at certain levels by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinks the most important thing would be to boost transparency of the market to avoid speculative moves," he said.\par \par \uc2 G8-SUMMIT/JAPAN (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090703e573000ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
