\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2009\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =BOJ UPS ECON VIEW; 2Q GDP MAY BE MARKEDLY BETTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan on Friday raised its assessment of the economy for the first time in nearly three years, as signs begin to emerge that Japan's recession may have passed its peak earlier this year. (published 0916 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OECD'S GURRIA: MORE GREEN SHOOTS SEEN IN US THAN IN EUROPE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Green shoots of economic recovery are more visible in the U.S. than in Europe thanks to larger stimulus efforts there, the head of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Friday. (published 0814 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OECD'S GURRIA: S&P WARNING ON UK VERY SURPRISING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development head Angel Gurria said Friday \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s warning that it could downgrade the U.K.'s triple-A credit rating was very surprising and underscored international ratings agencies' lack of credibility. (published 0932 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UK 1Q GDP UNREVISED, HOUSEHOLD SPENDING PLUNGES 1.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Plunging household expenditure and a record drawdown in inventories helped push the U.K. economy into an unrevised 1.9% contraction in the first quarter, the Office for National Statistics said Friday. (published 0928 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =ITALY MAR RETAIL SALES -5.2% ON YR; SHARPEST SINCE 97 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian retail sales in March fell at their fastest pace in more than a decade, as store sales - from food items to toys - plunged due to the recession, national statistics office Istat said Friday. (published 0931 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YOSANO:NOT THINKING OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENING\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WEAKEN YEN \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said Friday that he isn't planning to act to stop the yen's recent rise, prompting investors to lift the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a fresh two-month high against the dollar. (published 0137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRANCE MULLS NEW 2009 GDP FALL FORECAST OF 2.5%-3% - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French government is considering issuing a more optimistic official forecast about the likely contraction of its economy this year than recent public statements by senior ministers indicate, a person close to the government told Dow Jones Newswires Friday. (published 0838 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN MINISTRY:GOVT HAS RECEIVED 3 BIDS FOR GM'S OPEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Three bids have been submitted for \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Motors Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s German unit \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adam Opel AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  and it's now up to GM to decide which investor it favors before the government can assess any offer, German economics ministry spokesman Steffen Moritz told reporters Friday. (published 1014 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ GOV: APR-JUN GDP LIKELY TO PICK UP MARKEDLY VS JAN-MAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday the nation's gross domestic product in the April-June quarter will likely pick up markedly compared with the previous quarter, but he still warned economic downside risks and uncertainties remain high. (published 0721 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CHINA SEES INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT GROWTH PICKING UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's industrial production growth is accelerating and is likely to continue to do so through the balance of the year, a government ministry said. But growth remains below the torrid pace previously seen. (published 0909 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FED'S ROSENGREN: US IN "A SEVERE RECESSION" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. economy is in a "severe recession" but could begin to grow by the end of the year if financial markets continue healing, a top Federal Reserve official said Thursday. (published 2341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FED'S ROSENGREN: DEFLATION BIGGER CONCERN THAN INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prospect of deflation is a bigger concern right now than inflation, despite the huge expansion of the Federal Reserve's balance sheet to combat the recession, Federal Reserve Bank of Boston President Eric Rosengren said Thursday. (published 0034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FED'S PLOSSER: WANTS ASSETS ON FED BALANCE SHEET TO BE SHORT TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve would be best restricting its asset purchases to the shortest possible maturities, to ease the process of its eventual withdrawal of life support from the financial markets, Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia President Charles Plosser said Thursday. (published 0113 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FED'S PLOSSER: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NEEDS EXPLICIT INFLATION TARGETNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve needs stronger parameters, both on inflation and on its role independent from the U.S. government, a senior regional Fed official said Thursday. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE'S BLANCHFLOWER: HAVE PASSED MIDPOINT OF UK RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. has passed the midpoint of its economic recession, although the outlook for the jobs market remains "terrible," a member of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee said Thursday. (published 2230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE BEAN: TROUGH IN ECONOMIC ACTIVITY "MAY NOT BE TOO FAR OFF" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England Deputy Governor for Monetary Policy Charlie Bean said Thursday that a trough in U.K. economic activity might not be far away, and growth should start to return toward the end of this year. (published 2055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RUSSIA GOVT: BANKS SITUATION, BAD LOANS UNDER CONTROL -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian banks are unlikely to face another crisis similar to that of last fall and the government has the banking sector's non-performing loans under control, news agency Interfax reports First Deputy Prime Minister Igor Shuvalov as saying Friday. (published 1035 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    === DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY === \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ANOTHER GRIM YEAR FOR US REAL ESTATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real estate is where it all started - the boom and the bust - and real estate remains at the heart of the U.S. economy's prospects. (published 1016 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR OFF LOWS BUT STILL PRESSURED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is off multi-month lows seen in Asia Friday after markets spooked themselves into believing that the U.S. could go the same way as the U.K., which suffered Thursday a ratings outlook downgrade from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 0958 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par \par =BOE WATCH: MONEY SUPPLY DATA IN FOCUS AS BOND BUYS CONTINUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After being broadly ignored by investors for years, U.K. money supply data are gaining new prominence as a guide to how well the Bank of England's bond-buying program is working. (published 1052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(5/22) EDITORIAL: BRITAIN'S DEBT OMEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For a warning about America's fiscal future, consider yesterday's knews from Britain. United Kingdom stocks, bond futures and sterling all fell after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  lowered the country's credit outlook. (published 0431 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: UK POLITICAL SYSTEM KEY TO AVOIDING A DOWNGRADE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. faces the prospect of losing its long-held status as one of the world's highest rated borrowers. But its electoral system and a history of consensus building to overcome serious challenges give it a decent chance of avoiding that outcome. (published 1458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: DOLLAR TO SLIDE FURTHER STILL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following are forecasts for Friday's trading session. They may, at times, be at odds with the three-day and weekly trends in the table above. (published 0638 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: LOOK TOPPISH NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June bund and gilt futures are to decline further Friday and short-end futures also look toppish now. (published 0608 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ANOTHER GRIM YEAR FOR US REAL ESTATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real estate is where it all started - the boom and the bust - and real estate remains at the heart of the U.S. economy's prospects. (published 1116 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: DJIA LOSES GRIP ON DOLLAR; INFLATION RULES NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It used to be the case that if the U.S. stock market was rising, the dollar was probably falling, and vice versa. Not any more. (published 0510 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: SPEND IN HASTE, REPENT AT LEISURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The concerns of ratings agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  about the U.K.'s debt burden don't mean the country is on the cusp of losing its coveted AAA status, but they should serve as a warning to the leaders of key developed nations to think about the long-term repercussions of their current actions. (published 0007 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Isis Almeida, Dow Jones Newswires; +4420 7842 9375; isis.almeida@dowjones.com [ 22-05-09 1101GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090522e55m000fb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
