\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2009\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: BOJ RAISES ECON ASSESSMENT, WILL TAKE FOREIGN BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan on Friday raised its assessment of the economy for the first time in nearly three years, as signs begin to emerge that Japan's recession may have passed its peak earlier this year. (published 0456 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA SEES INDUS OUTPUT UP 8% IN 2Q, OVER 10% IN 2H \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's industry ministry forecast industrial production growth to accelerate from the second quarter, and said the government needs to improve the implementation of its stimulus plan to ensure that the rebound in industrial activity takes place. (published 0518 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YOSANO:NOT THINKING OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENING\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WEAKEN YEN \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said Friday that he isn't planning to act to stop the yen's recent rise, prompting investors to lift the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a fresh two-month high against the dollar. (published 0137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIA EYES MORE DERIVATIVES MKT TRANSPARENCY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's financial market regulators said Friday they will soon begin talks with market participants to improve transparency and other aspects of the country's over-the-counter, or OTC, derivative market. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: BANK INDONESIA: CPI TO EASE, RUPIAH TO KEEP GAINING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's annual inflation rate is likely to ease to around 5% in June, while there are no apparent inflationary pressures this month, a Bank Indonesia official said Friday. (published 0513 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SUSPECTED TO HAVE BOUGHT DOLLAR - DEALERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is believed to have again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday morning to slow the baht's appreciation, mirroring action it had likely taken for most of this week, dealers said. (published 0154 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM GOVT,CTRL BK AIM TO CURB WIDESPREAD USE OF DLR-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  and government agencies are determined to curb the widespread use of the dollar for transactions in the country, particularly in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City, state media reported Friday, citing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Nguyen Van Giau. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOUTH KOREA MIN: EXPECT NO QUICK REBOUND IN DOMESTIC ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean economy isn't likely to recover anytime soon despite some positive signs in recent economic data, according to Minister of Knowledge Economy Lee Youn-ho. (published 0138 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HKD PEG TO CNY RATHER THAN USD MAKES SENSE - MORGAN STANLEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Hong Kong dollar peg to the Chinese yuan makes sense because of the increasing economic ties between China and Hong Kong, but any decision by Hong Kong to scrap its U.S. dollar peg may happen only in the "considerably distant future," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Denise Yam said in a research report Friday. (published 0342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MOF: 4 LOCAL GOVTS SELL CNY16.9B 3-YEAR MUNICIPAL BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Four local governments in China sold a combined CNY16.9 billion worth of three-year municipal bonds at 1.67% Friday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said. (published 0417 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HAINAN AIR TO RAISE CNY3 BLN VIA PRIVATE PLACEMENT - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hainan Airlines Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . plans to raise CNY3 billion from a private placement of new shares to its parent company, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HNA Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and the Hainan provincial government, a person familiar with the matter told Dow Jones Newswires on Friday. (published 0219 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: DJIA LOSES GRIP ON DOLLAR; INFLATION RULES NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It used to be the case that if the U.S. stock market was rising, the dollar was probably falling, and vice versa. Not any more. (published 0510 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: AUSTRALIA MAY PRESSURE FUND MGRS TO CUT PENSION FEES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Political pressure is mounting in Australia for fund managers to reduce fees on pension products, a move that, while expected, has key players in the wealth management sector vulnerable while the market waits for the government's official stance on the issue. (published 0533 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: SPEND IN HASTE, REPENT AT LEISURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The concerns of ratings agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  about the U.K.'s debt burden don't mean the country is on the cusp of losing its coveted AAA status, but they should serve as a warning to the leaders of key developed nations to think about the long-term repercussions of their current actions. (published 0007 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: THAILAND 1Q GDP LIKELY SHRANK 6.3% ON YEAR -POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's gross domestic product likely continued to shrink in the first quarter of this year due to persistent falls in exports and inventory drawdowns, while domestic consumption and investment remained in the doldrums. (published 0553 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: MALAYSIA 1Q GDP LIKELY SHRANK FOR 1ST TIME IN 8 YRS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's trade-driven economy likely report its first economic contraction in nearly eight years after exports and manufacturing output plunged amid the global downturn, economists said. (published 0301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW:SINGAPORE APRIL CPI LIKELY +0.4% ON YR VS +1.6% MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's inflationary pressures likely eased further in April on weaker domestic economic activity and falling commodity prices. (published 0328 GMT) [ 22-05-09 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090522e55m00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
