\par\par \b \uc2 TOPWRAP 3-Europe's Juncker rebuffs Obama; Japan trade tumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 10:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Eurogroup's Juncker rebuffs Obama call for more spending\par \par \uc2 * German business sentiment still weak; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exports plunge\par \par \uc2 * U.S. Treasury seeks power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in failing firms\par \par \uc2 * IMF revamps lending, eases access to credit line\par \par \uc2 By James Mackenzie and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 PARIS/TOKYO, March 25 (Reuters) - A senior euro zone finance minister rebuffed U.S. calls for the region to ramp up government spending, saying on Wednesday that Europe was already doing enough to pump money into the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Just a week before the world's major leaders are due to meet in London to agree ways to defuse the crisis, Jean-Claude Juncker, chairman of the Eurogroup forum of euro zone finance ministers, dismissed President Barack Obama's calls for leading economies to spend more in concerted action.\par \par \uc2 A record drop in Japanese exports underlined the scale of the global collapse in spending by companies and consumers. The world's second largest economy reported exports nearly halved in February from a year earlier, a record fall, and imports also tumbled.\par \par \uc2 The closely-watched Ifo gauge showed business sentiment also remained weak in Germany, Europe's biggest economy. The main business climate index slipped to 82.1 in March from 82.6 in February and the Ifo institute said companies did not believe the bottom of the downturn had been reached yet.\par \par \uc2 President Obama renewed calls on Tuesday for leading economies to boost stimulus spending, repair credit markets and extend aid to poor countries when Group of 20 leaders meet in London on April 2.\par \par \uc2 But Juncker retorted: "The European stimulus plans are strong, they are demanding and they are significant in terms of volume and quality.\par \par \uc2 "There is no question that, upon the demand of the United States, that we would increase it," he told Europe 1 radio.\par \par \uc2 European governments say their welfare safety nets automatically pump more money into their economies, on top of the formal stimulus packages they have announced.\par \par \uc2 BANKING POWERS\par \par \uc2 The global stock market rally sparked by Washington's plan to soak up the bad and doubtful debts at the root of the crisis was faltering on Wednesday as investors turned cautious.\par \par \uc2 Europe's leading shares edged up 0.2 percent while Japan's Nikkei index ended barely changed\par \par \uc2 More jobs in the UK's once booming financial services sector were slashed, with Europe's biggest bank \uc2 HSBC\uc2  announcing 1,200 job cuts. Insurer \uc2 Legal and General\uc2  said it expected its workforce to shrink this year by much the same amount as last year's 10 percent cut.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. administration sought more powers on Tuesday to wind down failed non-banking financial institutions to avoid costly bailouts in the future.\par \par \uc2 "We will recover from this recession. But it will take time, it will take patience," Obama said in the opening statement of his second prime-time White House news conference since he took office on Jan. 20. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.whitehouse.gov/blog/09/03/24/A-time-for-global-action/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.whitehouse.gov/blog/09/03/24/A-time-for-global-action/}}}\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have recently cited glimmers of improvement in a battered housing market, whose collapse set off the credit market meltdown and the worst global recession since the Great Depression of the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has also been making encouraging comments about China's prospects and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser joined on Wednesday a chorus of officials suggesting the world's third-biggest economy was bottoming out from a sharp slowdown late last year.\par \par \uc2 With several emerging economies in Europe and elsewhere among the hardest hit by the crisis, the International Monetary Fund revamped its lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and created a new credit line for well-run economies.\par \par \uc2 So far, the IMF has loaned $50 billion to a string of countries including Iceland, Hungary, Ukraine, Latvia, Belarus, Serbia and Pakistan. On Wednesday it said it had agreed a 20 billion euro aid package with Romania.\par \par \uc2 One of the main items on the G20 agenda for next week's meeting will be extra funding for the IMF.\par \par \uc2 China and Russia, two of the world's main holders of big foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, are also pushing the idea of an alternative world reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, voicing concern that Obama's massive bailout packages could weaken the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Obama dismissed the idea. "I don't believe that there's a need for a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, adding the dollar was "extraordinarily strong".\par \par \uc2 He also played down fears of a U.S.-European rift on the world economic action plan, saying the G20 summit should be able to agree on common goals of spurring world growth, revamping financial regulations and avoiding trade protectionism. (Reporting by Reuters bureaus worldwide; Writing by Ruth Pitchford, editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 3, PIX, GRAPHIC)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090328e53p0006j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgo