\par\par \b \uc2 TOPWRAP 2-Obama sees progress on crisis; Japan trade tumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 1,018 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Obama says recovery efforts start yielding results\par \par \uc2 * U.S. Treasury seeks power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in failing firms\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports plunge, investor sentiment hits record low\par \par \uc2 * IMF revamps lending, eases access to credit line\par \par \uc2 * Obama rejects calls for alternative world reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Matt Spetalnick and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON/TOKYO, March 25 (Reuters) - President Barack Obama tried to assure Americans his team's effort to revive the U.S. economy was starting to bear fruit just as Japan's crumbling exports underscored the depth of the world recession.\par \par \uc2 Obama's assurances followed his plea for more concerted action to tackle the global crisis and came just a day after Washington's plan to soak up toxic assets at the root of the global financial meltdown stoked a worldwide stock market rally.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. administration also sought more powers to wind down failed non-banking financial institutions to avoid costly bailouts in the future.\par \par \uc2 In an article published around the world on Tuesday, Obama called on leading economies to enact robust stimulus spending, repair credit markets and extend aid to poor countries when Group of 20 leaders meet in London late next week.\par \par \uc2 He also said Washington was prepared to spearhead this effort, doing its part to pull the world's biggest economy out of its deep 15-month old downturn. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.whitehouse.gov/blog/09/03/24/A-time-for-global-actio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.whitehouse.gov/blog/09/03/24/A-time-for-global-actio}}}\uc2  n/) "We will recover from this recession. But it will take time, it will take patience," Obama said in the opening statement of his second prime-time White House news conference since he took office on Jan. 20.\par \par \uc2 "We've put in place a comprehensive strategy designed to attack this crisis on all fronts. It's a strategy to create jobs, to help responsible homeowners, to restart lending, and to grow our economy over the long-term. And we are beginning to see signs of progress."\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have recently cited glimmers of improvement in a battered housing market, whose collapse set off the credit market meltdown and the worst global recession since the Great Depression of the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has also been making encouraging comments about China's prospects and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser joined on Wednesday a chorus of officials suggesting the world's third-biggest economy was bottoming out from a sharp slowdown late last year.\par \par \uc2 Yet most key economic indicators still suggest a long and painful recession for the U.S. and world economy and no near-term relief for export-dependent nations such as Japan.\par \par \uc2 The world's second-largest economy reported exports nearly halved in February from a year earlier, a record fall, and imports also tumbled, a sign both domestic consumption and demand from key U.S. and European markets were crumbling.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the gloom, a gauge of Japanese retail equity investors' sentiment based on a Reuters poll fell for the third straight month in March to its lowest reading since the survey began in January 2006.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan deputy governor Hirohide Yamaguchi acknowledged Japan suffered more than its peers and suggested the bank's near-term focus would be on stabilising markets and easing corporate funding strains.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, Germany's key business sentiment gauge, the Ifo index due at 0900 GMT on Wednesday, is expected to inch down in March, with its expectations component seen rising a notch, suggesting some stabilisation, albeit at very low levels.\par \par \uc2 NEW POWERS\par \par \uc2 Obama's high approval ratings have been tested by public outrage over payment of $165 million in executive bonuses by \uc2 American International Group\uc2  after the insurance giant was rescued with $180 billion in taxpayer bailout funds.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, his Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, backed by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke, appealed to lawmakers to grant the government more power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in troubled firms in order to avert AIG-style debacles in the future.\par \par \uc2 Obama took the podium after U.S. stocks slid while investors paused to reassess the government's plan to purge bank balance sheets of up to $1 trillion in troubled assets. Initial euphoria over the plan had driven U.S. stocks sharply higher on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets initially slipped on cue from Wall Street, but later pared losses with Tokyo's stocks ending barely changed and the rest of Asia-Pacific slightly positive.\par \par \uc2 The AIG furore distracted from Obama's effort to convince lawmakers and the public that his $787 billion economic stimulus package will jolt the economy out of recession and his record $3.55 trillion budget for fiscal 2010 will be money well spent.\par \par \uc2 Concerns that record fiscal deficits and the Fed's hefty liquidity injections will create a global glut of dollars, made the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffer its biggest weekly loss since 1985 last week and revived calls for an alternative world reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Obama brushed off such proposals recently brought up by China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 "I don't believe that there's a need for a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, adding the dollar was "extraordinarily strong.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the dollar dipped against the euro, but held on to most of the gains made the previous day on profit-taking after last week's slide.\par \par \uc2 Obama also played down fears of a U.S.-European rift on the world economic action plan, saying the G20 summit should be able to agree on common goals of spurring world growth, revamping financial regulations and avoiding trade protectionism.\par \par \uc2 European leaders have rejected U.S. pleas for more spending on top of the trillions of dollars governments have already pledged, fearing it would with many arguing the priority was to strengthen market regulation.\par \par \uc2 With several emerging economies in Europe and elsewhere among the hardest hit by the crisis, the International Monetary Fund revamped its lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and created a new credit line for well-run economies. (Reporting by Reuters bureaus worldwide; Writing by Tomasz Janowski; Editing by Jean Yoon)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 2, PIX, GRAPHIC)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090328e53p0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
