\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - TOPWRAP 6-UK unemployment at 2 mln; Fed statement awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 996 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 20:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects UK uemployment data in paragraph 9)\par \par \uc2 * Federal Reserve policy statement awaited\par \par \uc2 * British unemployment highest for 12 years\par \par \uc2 * U.S. 2008 Q4 current account narrows sharply\par \par \uc2 * BOJ boosts govt bond buying; holds rates, JGBs up\par \par \uc2 * ECB's Trichet sees hope for 2010; 2009 will be very hard\par \par \uc2 (For more on the financial crisis, click [nCRISIS])\par \par \uc2 By Nick Olivari and Ralph Boulton\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/LONDON, March 18 (Reuters) - British unemployment rose to its highest in 12 years on Wednesday, pointing to a longer, deeper recession than expected, as investors around the world awaited a statement from the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concluding a two-day policy setting meeting later on Wednesday. While it is widely expected to vow it will do whatever it takes to return the U.S. economy to health, investors are aware it may not announce any concrete steps even as Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to push money into the country's moribund economy. [ID:nN16520160]\par \par \uc2 The Fed has exhausted its main economic spur in lowering interest rates to between zero and 0.25 percent, and many expect it will soon follow Japan and Britain in pumping money directly into the system.\par \par \uc2 The Fed "will probably again raise the possibility of buying Treasuries," said Benedikt Germanier, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Stamford, Connecticut. But "it could turn out to be a non-event or even a disappointment because it won't be much different" from the statement from the January meeting.\par \par \uc2 U.S. benchmark stock indexes fell <.N> amid caution ahead of the forthcoming Fed announcement with the Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> down 1.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the ECB was ready to take additional measures to tackle the global economic crisis even as he suggested a recovery could be in sight.\par \par \uc2 "The year 2009 will be very, very difficult," Trichet told Europe 1 radio. "At the same time, there is quite general agreement between all public and private institutions that 2010 may be the year of moderate recovery in growth."\par \par \uc2 Underlying economic figures remained grim.\par \par \uc2 Britain registered February unemployment over two million for the first time since 1997, reinforcing fears the country's downturn could be worse than previously thought. The number of people claiming jobless benefit rose in February by the biggest amount on record, official data showed. [ID:nL1393856]\par \par \uc2 European shares fell, with the pan-regional FTSEurofirst 300 <.FTEU3> down 1.6 percent and Britain's FTSE 100 <.FTSE> falling 2.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "There is no hiding place and they need to do everything within their power to get the economy moving. Hopefully we won't be talking about 3 million out of work in 12 months time," said Rob Pike, head of trading at Shortsandlongs.com.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, government data showed the U.S. current account for the fourth quarter of 2008 narrowing sharply and by more than expected to $132.8 billion, the smallest since 2003 and a reflection of a plunge in trade across the world. [ID;nN1724469]\par \par \uc2 BOJ ACTS\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan surprised markets by raising annual government bond purchases by 29 percent to a record $219 billion to "smooth market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The move was more than analysts had expected and sooner than many had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 As world political leaders prepare for a G20 summit to help coordinate their efforts to overcome the crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leadership has been accused of sluggishness in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to revive money supply to feed industry and trade.\par \par \uc2 "Today's announcement shows the BOJ is doing more than what the markets expected to strengthen monetary easing," said Junko Nishioka, chief Japan economist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 While the Bank of Japan has been buying government debt for decades, other central banks have only started considering such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  after pushing interest rates near to zero.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England started buying government bonds with newly created money on March 11 and the Federal Reserve has mentioned such a scheme as a step it could take in the face of the worst global downturn since the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 FED ON HOLD?\par \par \uc2 Few analysts expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adopt such a scheme on Wednesday, but speculation it may suggest going that route in the future propped up the U.S. Treasury bond market. [MKTS/GLOB]\par \par \uc2 "The markets will be particularly focused on whether or not the Fed advances its commentary regarding quantitative easing in the form of outright Treasury purchases," said Joseph LaVorgna, chief U.S. economist at Deutsche Bank Securities in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has said the economy can start recovering next year if the authorities succeed in pulling through the complex and costly clean-up of the shattered financial sector.\par \par \uc2 The health of the financial industry is seen as key to the recovery in the United States and elsewhere, but bailing out institutions blamed by many for unleashing the crisis with reckless risk-taking has proved a tough sell.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, in charge of a $700 billion financial sector rescue, has his hands full confronting public outrage at fat bonuses taxpayer-financed \uc2 American International Group Inc\uc2  awarded its employees.\par \par \uc2 AIG is surviving on three federal bailouts worth up to $180 billion, but it paid out $165 million in bonuses. AIG Chief Executive Officer Edward Liddy on Wednesday gave no indication the insurer would pull back the bonuses. [ID:nWBT010880]\par \par \uc2 U.S. Representative Barney Frank, chairman of the House of Representatives Financial Services committee, said at a hearing on the bailout that it was time for the United States to assert its ownership rights over AIG and called the bonuses "wholly unjustified." (Reporting by Reuters bureaux worldwide; Writing by Nick Olivari)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 6, PIX, GRAPHIC, CORRECTED)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|MNI|J|Z|RNP|DNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090318e53i0017x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
