\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan says  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  stability needed for economy\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2009\par 03:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click) (Adds BOJ's monthly report)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 20 (Reuters) - Japan needs to help ensure exchange rates are stable for its economy to recover, its new finance minister said, as slumping global demand batters exports and pushes the nation deeper into recession.\par \par \uc2 A rapid rise in the yen, which cuts into the value of overseas earnings when repatriated, has only rubbed salt into wounds for manufacturers such as \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  and \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 , many of whom have fallen deep into the red and are restructuring as their main markets contract sharply.\par \par \uc2 Data this week showed Japan's economy shrank at an annual rate of 12.7 percent in the last quarter -- its fastest rate since the first oil crisis in 1974, and three times the fall in gross domestic product in the same quarter in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscored corporate pain in its monthly report on Friday, reiterating that company profits were deteriorating faster as exports and capital investment fall sharply.\par \par \uc2 "First of all, we need to ensure Japan's exchange rates are stable," Finance Minister Kaoru Yosano told parliament.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has climbed almost 15 percent versus the dollar and 30 percent against the euro since the collapse of U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in September as investors dumped carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Japan's low interest rates had made the yen popular for years as a source of cheap funds for carry trades -- borrowing the yen to buy higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 The yen, however, has weakened to around 94 to the dollar from a 13-\u189\'00\'BD year high of 87.10 hit last month as investors fret about the fast-deteriorating economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, after a 15-month-long, 35 trillion yen ($371.7 billion) selling spree aimed at preventing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength from snuffing out an economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Yosano, who is also economics minister, also said that a global policy response was necessary to stem the widening fallout from the worldwide financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to imagine a situation where Japan alone recovers," he said.\par \par \uc2 Yosano replaced Shoichi Nakagawa, who resigned as finance minister on Tuesday after being forced to deny he was drunk at a G7 news conference in Rome over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, deciding whether and when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  implements it. ($1=94.16 Yen) (Additional reporting by Rika Otsuka; Editing by Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090220e52k0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
