\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Japan Fin Min:G7 Nations Against Volatile Exchange Rates\b0\par\par\uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2009\par 20:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Adds more comments and background.) \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROME (Dow Jones)--Possibly in an attempt to keep investors from pushing up the yen too much, Japan's finance minister stressed on Saturday that the Group of Seven leading industrial powers are ready to collaborate to fight volatile exchange rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The warning from Shoichi Nakagawa came after the G7 finance officials wrapped up their two-day meeting by issuing a statement that cautioned against exchange-rate volatility that could further damage the beleaguered global economy.\par \par \uc2 During a joint news conference with Nakagawa, Bank of Japan Governor. Masaaki Shirakawa also highlighted that the group has a renewed determination to take whatever actions needed to put the ongoing global economic crisis to rest. Moreover, Shirakawa described the state of Japan's recession-plagued economy as "very severe," adding to speculation that the bank may mull more stimulus measures if the economy demands them.\par \par \uc2 The stance shown by Japan's top policymakers suggests they're eager to work with other economies to limit the depth of the global slump, while they want no further market problems such as sharp yen rises. With Japan heavily dependent on exports for growth, Nakagawa also urged his G7 peers never to succumb to the temptation of protectionist measures that could choke off global trade, according to a finance ministry official.\par \par \uc2 "I want (market participants) to think about the significance of the fact that the G7 inserted language in their communique saying they will corporate in countering rapid movements in exchange rates," Nakagawa said at the press conference.\par \par \uc2 The G7 rich nations in their closing statement said "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates have adverse implications for economic and financial stability" and that they'll "cooperate as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 To be sure, Nakagawa's rhetoric wasn't necessarily sharper than his recent remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't about to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen for dollars at the current levels of Y92. But his words still demonstrate his nagging concerns about potential yen surges.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise makes Japan's exports more expensive abroad and reduces the yen value of profits earned in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That's a frightening prospect for a country whose exports fell by a record 35.0% on year in December. With demand abroad crumbling, Japan's industrial output by a record 9.6% on month in December. As manufacturers fire workers to stay afloat, the nation's jobless rate jumped 0.5 point to 4.4% in that month - the biggest increase in about four decades.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar last year sank 19.15% against the yen to Y90.63. On Jan. 21 this year, the dollar hit a 13-and-a-half-year low of Y87.10. Those levels are far below the average profit-or-loss break-even rate of around Y101 to Y102 for Japan's exporters estimated by Toshihiro Nagahama, chief economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister said Tokyo's priority is to pass budget bills to empower the government to carry out its promised fiscal stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "Though the opposition groups are staging silly resistance, the government first needs to have its fiscal 2009 budget as well as relevant legislation for a supplementary budget (for the current fiscal year ending March) enacted," Nakagawa said. Passing those bills "will be the greatest economic countermeasure."\par \par \uc2 Still, the general market view is that the three stimulus packages assembled by the government since August, valued at Y75 trillion, is too little to turn around Japan's economy given rapid falls in its exports.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa, in the meantime, said he will carefully examine data ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's planned policy meeting on Monday and Tuesday. But "at this stage, there's little I can say about specifics."\par \par \uc2 As central banks around the globe race to cut borrowing costs to underpin their economies, the Bank of Japan has also kept its rates at a rock-bottom 0.1% since December. It has taken what it calls "extraordinary" steps to help banks and companies in trouble, including commitments to buy up to Y3 trillion in highly rated commercial paper and Y1 trillion in stocks held by local banks.\par \par \uc2 "The G7 didn't necessarily refer to the Bank of Japan ... but I think its assessment is that those kinds of measures (taken by the bank) are appropriate," Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2 Nakagawa also said he "highly values" the bank's actions.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 14-02-09 1928GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090214e52e0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
