\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japan Likely To Hold Fire, Even As Yen Crushes Econ\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 1,232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2009\par 08:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Even as a strong yen crushes Japan's export-dependent economy, the Japanese government likely won't try to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down - probably because \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't work, say analysts.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notorious for its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when a yen surge nearly throttled its sickly economy earlier this decade, holding fire as the yen soars entails heavy sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 Exports fell a record 35% in December from a year earlier. Industrial output is in a tailspin. Manufacturers are on to the rocks: electronics giant \uc2 Hitachi Ltd\uc2 . forecasts Japan's biggest-ever corporate loss this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 But analysts say there are compelling reasons why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five years - will remain out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, at least not for now.\par \par \uc2 For one, it wouldn't be an easy fight. Market observes say some players are positioning for local investors to move huge amounts of capital back to Japan as they sell off depreciated foreign assets and square their books ahead of the March 31 fiscal-year end. That's adding to upward pressure on the yen, which has also been driven higher by speculation that global investors are unwinding bets financed with borrowed yen.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be taking on a difficult battle given the yen-repatriation flows ahead of fiscal year-end and the unremitting bouts of risk aversion that continue to support the yen," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Then there's politics, said Claudio Piron, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the global economic downturn raising the risks that some countries may pursue protectionist trade policies - and strong warnings from the International Monetary Fund against such policies - it would be difficult for Japan to push its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down at the expense of its trading partners, like the U.S. and Europe. Such a move could act as a catalyst for competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations by other countries, such as China, Piron said.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa said Tuesday that when Group of Seven finance ministers meet this weekend, he wants to focus discussions on countering protectionist sentiment brewing in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Washington, meanwhile, appears to remain opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asked last month before he became Treasury secretary about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, Timothy Geithner said flexible exchange rates are the best system for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Another reason for Japan not to step in is that the global economic context is very different now from that of March 2004, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ending a 15-month campaign that sold Y35 trillion, mostly for dollars, says Tim Condon, head of Asian research at ING.\par \par \uc2 Back then, the global economy was in an upswing and Japan was unique in having ultra-low interest rates. Now, however, central banks from the U.S. and the U.K. to Taiwan are converging policy rates to levels close to zero, lowering the relative appeal of overseas assets against yen-denominated assets.\par \par \uc2 For Japanese officials, who routinely stress that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates ought to reflect economic fundamentals, that development takes some of the air out of any argument that a weaker yen is warranted.\par \par \uc2 What's more, analysts say it's not clear if Japan, on its own or even jointly with others, would be able to put a lasting dent in the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Japan could print a theoretically unlimited amount of yen to dump on the market. But that's not likely. With $1 trillion worth or \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves already, Japan probably isn't keen to load up on more dollars.\par \par \uc2 Anything less than massive, sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, likely wouldn't be sufficient to push the dollar higher against the yen in a such a huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where daily turnover reaches $3.2 trillion, according to the Bank of International Settlements latest report on the issue, released in 2007. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japan's Economy On The Rocks \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not that Japan Inc. hasn't sounded the alarm. Vice Finance Minister Kazuyuki Sugimoto last month warned against "excessive movement" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Japan's most powerful business lobby, Nippon Keidanren, has reportedly urged the government to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen started to soar against the dollar at the worst possible moment - just when the global economy took a dive, led by the U.S. financial crash last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This move came just when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of many rival exporting nations were tumbling, eroding the relative competitiveness of Japan's exporters. Over the last six months, as the dollar posted sizable gains versus most other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including a 35% jump against the South Korean won and a 16% rise against the Indian rupee, the greenback lost 17% against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has regained some ground against the yen, rising just under 2% so far this year. It traded at Y91.39 at 0714 GMT Tuesday, well above the Y87.11 mark hit Jan 21, its lowest since 1995. Still, the yen remains too strong for Japanese exporters to stay competitive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey of Japanese exporters by Japan's Cabinet Office released near the beginning of the current fiscal year showed that their average break-even dollar exchange rate was Y104.7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's tankan survey Dec. 15 revealed that large Japanese manufacturing companies were looking for the greenback to average Y101 in the six months to March 31. In fact the yen has been stronger than that since early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ING forecasts the dollar will fall to Y85 this quarter before recovering to around Y100 by the year end, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  forecasts the rate at Y85 by the end of June and Y90 by the end of December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates the risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen is now at 37%, the highest in four years, based on a model that takes into account the momentum of exchange rates and economic growth differentials, among other factors. Under the same model, the yen's real exchange rate against a variety of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is 14% above historical levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  still isn't likely, analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  reckon a combination of bad economic data and continued yen demand will eventually compel Japan to act.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With concerns emerging in some quarters about excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  corrections since the start of the financial crisis... we believe there is still room for other countries to accept \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at stemming the rapid rise in the yen," Goldman analysts Tetsufumi Yamakawa, Yuriko Tanaka and Chiwoong Lee said in a recent report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ken McCallum in Tokyo contributed to this story.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 10, 2009 02:39 ET (07:39 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090820e52a0my8f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
