\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan sees possible  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  debate at Rome G7\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara   \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2009\par 03:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 10 (Reuters) - Some Group of Seven nations may raise the issue of exchange rates at a weekend meeting in Rome, a senior official at Japan's finance ministry said, although he offered few clues on whether Tokyo will complain about the damage a strong yen is doing to its export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 G7 finance leaders will agree to act promptly to support economies hurt by the global financial turmoil, and fiscal sustainability will also be debated as they have become more alert to the issue of unwinding huge spending plans, he said.\par \par \uc2 "They won't say it aloud when they are striving to stimulate their economies, but each country is becoming very conscious about how to exit these policies," the official told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States meet in Rome on Friday and Saturday as they try to find a solution to the deepening credit crunch.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European bond yields have risen on fears of huge debt issuance as policymakers dish out trillions of dollars in bailouts and fiscal stimulus packages to tackle recession.\par \par \uc2 Some nations may talk about exchange rates in discussions on the global economy, as is always the case, the official said, adding that he had no preset idea on what the G7 communique would say on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 Asked if Japan will take up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues at the G7 meeting, the official said: "That depends on how discussions on overall macroeconomic conditions develop."\par \par \uc2 Japan, like the United States, is in recession and can ill afford a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which puts an extra choke-hold on exporters that are cutting jobs and shuttering factories in the face of a global slump in demand.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has let off verbal volleys against sharp rises in the yen, but so far Japanese officials have stopped short of saying they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and many market players do not believe they will.\par \par \uc2 The G7 nations have stuck to their stance that excessive exchange-rate moves are undesirable without singling out a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  until October last year, when they issued an unusual separate statement warning against sharp rises in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Despite a couple of bouts of additional strength since then, the yen's exchange rate heading into this weekend's G7 is almost exactly where it was before the statement, at around 91 yen per dollar.\par \par \uc2 MESSAGE AGAINST PROTECTIONISM\par \par \uc2 Japan expects to hear from U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner the outlook for the U.S. economy and what measures it is taking to tackle the crisis when he meets Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa on Friday on the sidelines of the G7 meeting, the official said.\par \par \uc2 Investors are keeping a close eye out on developments over a U.S. bank bailout plan, expected to be unveiled by Geithner on Tuesday, and stimulus packages to help the distressed economy.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo also hopes the G7 sends a clear message against trade protectionism, which Nakagawa has said will be taken up in Rome.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Barack Obama's multi-billion dollar stimulus package, which still requires congressional approval, generated concern in some countries, who fear its "Buy American" clause is protectionist.\par \par \uc2 Jitters over the plight of U.S. and European banks have prompted risk-averse investors to hoard the yen, pushing it to a 13-1/2-year high of 87.10 yen against the dollar last month. The dollar has since regained some ground and stood around 91.30 yen on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The yen rally has hammered exports and pushed the world's No.2 economy deeper into recession, with big exporters such as \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  and \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  slashing jobs and cutting output to cope.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy is expected to have suffered its biggest contraction since 1974 in the final three months of last year, a Reuters poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Japan has not stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since a 15-month yen selling spree that ended in 2004 in which it sold 35 trillion yen ($383 billion) to prevent yen strength from snuffing out an economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry sets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, deciding whether and when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  implements it. ($1=91.48 Yen) (Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 G7/JAPAN (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090210e52a00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
