\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan may help U.S. if China stops debt purchase\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Olivari   \par \uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2009\par 22:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Japan could be a counterweight against rising U.S. borrowing costs should China buy less U.S. government debt in response to pressure from Washington to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Investors are on full alert that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  could buy dollars to bring the yen down from a 13-1/2- year peak first touched in December and hit again in January.\par \par \uc2 Japan, which relies heavily on trade to power its economy, saw its exports plunge 35 percent in December.\par \par \uc2 Already holding $1.03 trillion in official reserve assets, according to International Monetary Fund data, the next question is what would \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  do with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars.\par \par \uc2 Given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the second-biggest holder of U.S. debt after China, it would likely buy Treasuries, denting some of the impact if Beijing hits back over recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  criticism by Timothy Geithner, now Treasury secretary.\par \par \uc2 "Japan gets a higher dollar/yen rate and keeps the domestic exporters happy while the increased supply of U.S. debt and specifically Treasuries gets mopped up," said Dustin Reid, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Global Banking & Markets, in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. government "could issue a lot more debt and it is no secret that Japan wants its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken," Reid said.\par \par \uc2 Prices of U.S. Treasury bonds fell last week, partly on concerns that Geithner's comments, made in testimony to senators weighing his nomination as Treasury secretary, could provoke China into buying less U.S. debt.\par \par \uc2 But there was no sign of weaker foreign demand at a $40 billion auction of two-year Treasury notes on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN'S INCENTIVE\par \par \uc2 Foreign central banks and individuals hold more than half of the $5.8 trillion in outstanding U.S. government debt. China and Japan hold about a quarter of the total amount.\par \par \uc2 China edged out Japan as the biggest holder of U.S. debt in 2008 after years of recycling the money they received from U.S. consumers for exports by buying Treasuries. That began a cycle of keeping the U.S. consumer at the mall on cheap loans.\par \par \uc2 Amid the global downturn, Japan has no less incentive to keep its factories running.\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. government set to issue some $2 trillion of debt in 2009 alone in order to fund a stimulus package to revive the economy, Washington is keen to see buyers.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably result in increased purchases of U.S. debt," said Omer Esiner, senior market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "Any decrease in demand for U.S. debt by China that is offset by Japan would be good for the U.S. economy."\par \par \uc2 Shortly before Geithner won confirmation as U.S. Treasury secretary on Monday, the White House hedged his statement last week -- that President Barack Obama believed China was manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- by saying a formal decision on the issue would be made in coming months.\par \par \uc2 The fear, though, is that this could be an opening salvo in a more confrontational relationship between the United States and China.\par \par \uc2 China has denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation charge, but IMF Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn said on Monday the yuan was "significantly undervalued."\par \par \uc2 YUAN VS YEN\par \par \uc2 In the first three years after China let it begin to appreciate in July 2005 in response to long criticism from U.S. lawmakers, the yuan gained around 18 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the initial revaluation.\par \par \uc2 But today, it is trading at 6.83 yuan to the dollar, kept by Chinese authorities at roughly where it was in July 2008.\par \par \uc2 At those levels, China has sold vastly more goods to the United States than the latter has sold to China. While China does have a cheap labor force, its trade advantage over other low-cost producers is bolstered by the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Between January and November 2008, the United States ran a trade gap with China of $246.45 billion, according to U.S. data, after a $256.2 billion deficit in 2007 as a a whole.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the U.S. deficit with Japan was $67.39 billion for the first 11 months of 2008, after an $82.76 billion deficit in 2007. The freely floated yen last traded around 89.50 yen to the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen by buying dollars could also be considered a "manipulation" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Washington will point no fingers there, given the fundamentally different relationship with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does have a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they never (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) without alerting the Fed," said Peter Zeihan, vice president of analysis at Stratfor, an Austin, Texas-based global intelligence company. "Independent of that, Japan is an ally."\par \par \uc2 To be sure, whether the United States and China clash outright is still open to debate, as is whether Japan would even be able to press an advantage.\par \par \uc2 While Japan would probably like the dollar/yen rate to be above 100 instead of the current 89.14, the impact of China not buying U.S. Treasuries or moving reserves into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could overwhelm any other actions, said Joseph Trevisani, chief market analyst at Saddle River, New-Jersey based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Solutions.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not sure we would get the type of logical follow-up effects because of the turmoil," Trevisani said. "Fear for the U.S. economy could overwhelm the dollar and it would fall." (Reporting by Nick Olivari; Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090127e51r001p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
