\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Mid-Term Bonds Rise, Others Fall On BOJ Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 827 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2009\par 08:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   At 0600 GMT   Change \par    TFX Mar 3-Mos Euroyen Price:      99.375    -0.020 \par    TSE Mar 10-Yr JGB Futures Price:  139.81    +0.170 \par    10-Yr 1.3% JGB No. 298 Yield:     1.230%    +0.000 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Bonds in the middle of the JGB curve rose, while those at both ends fell, as the market digested the Bank of Japan's plans to step up its JGB purchases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 With few upcoming domestic developments to give direction early next week, market players will focus on U.S. and European markets, seeking clues to how serious the banking system problems are overseas and how the likelihood of sharply increased issuance will affect the U.S. Treasury market. "The focus continues to be on financial system issues, especially in Europe, plus \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mitsubishi UFJ Securities strategist Naomi Hasegawa said.\par \par \uc2 Comments Thursday by U.S. Treasury Secretary-nominee Timothy Geithner on alleged Chinese manipulation of the yuan may make it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the yen continues to rise. That could force the BOJ to take additional quantitative easing steps, which in turn would likely bring down short-end rates and JGB yields, Hasegawa said.\par \par \uc2 The biggest market mover on Friday was the BOJ's JGB purchasing, or rinban, plan announced after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board meeting Thursday. The distribution by tenor surprised JGB players, who were expecting most of the purchasing to be concentrated in the back end of the yield curve in an attempt to bring down long-term borrowing rates.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the BOJ announced that its buys would be concentrated in the one-year to 10-year sector of the yield curve, with fewer than expected in the 10-30-year zone or in maturities of less than one year.\par \par \uc2 As many analysts predicted before the market opened, that caused the JGB yield curve to steepen.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, lead March JGB futures - which correspond to seven-year bonds - added 0.17 to 139.81, and the yield on five-year cash bonds fell 1 basis point to 0.665% as of 0600 GMT, its lowest point since September 2005.\par \par \uc2 Other cash bonds were sold. The two-year yield gained 0.5 basis point to 0.36%, the 20-year yield rose 2.5 basis points to 1.865% and the 20-year yield gained 3 basis points to 1.93%.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year bond was underwater most of the day, but gained before the close to finish flat at a 1.23% yield by 0600 GMT.\par \par \uc2 For superlong bonds especially, "the problem is that from February they'll be buying only once a month in that sector, so it's less flexible, and for dealers there's less occasion to use BOJ rinban \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a hedge," \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  strategist Tatsuo Ichikawa said.\par \par \uc2 The reason for crafting the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that way, he said, is that "the BOJ wants to have better management of its JGB holdings. They don't want to have too many short-end or long-end JGBs."\par \par \uc2 "From the beginning of the morning session, all investors we've seen have been sellers," \uc2 Nikko Citigroup\uc2  head JGB trader Hideki Nakano said. That back-up in long-end yields could draw dip-buyers, he said, but noted that market players might be wary of buying superlong bonds ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's auction of Y900 billion in new 20-year bonds next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Aside from the BOJ rinban \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other factors may come into play soon that could shake JGBs out of their recent range.\par \par \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Securities strategist Jun Ishi noted that since late November the lead JGB contract has only briefly broken out of a narrow 138.50-140.00 range. Meanwhile, the 10-year cash bond yield has been stuck between 1.2% and 1.3% since late December.\par \par \uc2 Both will soon break out of their ranges, Ishii predicted, reckoning that "futures will probably decline and the benchmark yield rise."\par \par \uc2 He cited three likely factors: Domestic investors could sell JGBs to take profit and cover losses on other instruments; foreigners' liquidation selling "might gain momentum ...because there will be pent-up demand for fund cancellations at the end of March"; and "hope for Obamanomics" will spark an unwinding of flight-to-quality trades and growing concern about the U.S. Treasury supply/demand balance.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, dire economic indicators raise the prospect that fourth-quarter GDP could contract by 10% or more, which normally would be considered a bond-buying factor.\par \par \uc2 However, Ishii noted, "if the JGB market has already factored in the economic contraction...there might actually be some relief with the GDP news," creating a fourth reason to sell bonds.\par \par \uc2 -By Michael S. Arnold, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-5255-2929; michael.arnold@dowjones.com [ 23-01-09 0710GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1037 4153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090123e51n00088\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
