\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,088 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2009\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:JAPAN NOV MACHINERY ORDERS POST RECORD 16.2% DROP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese machinery orders fell at their fastest rate on record in November as the global economic crisis severely dented manufacturers' appetite for investment, adding to fears that the country's recession will worsen. (published 0415 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIA SHEDS FEW JOBS IN DEC, WORSE TO COME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy shed just 1,200 jobs in December, well short of the expected 20,000 loss, lifting hopes that the labor market will remain resilient in the face of the global financial crisis, and removing some of the pressure for further hefty interest rate cuts. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA 08 ACTUAL FDI UP NEARLY 24%; OUTLOOK DIFFICULT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's actual foreign direct investment rose nearly 24% to $92.40 billion in 2008, rising for the third straight year to a record high, but the worsening economic slowdown continued to keep investment inflows in check. (published 0548 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: INDIA WPI +5.24% ON YEAR IN WEEK ENDED JAN 3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's inflation rate eased to 5.24% in the week ended Jan. 3 from 5.91% in the previous week, the government said Friday. (published 0548 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =NZ AVG DEC HOUSE PRICES -2.7% VS NOV - REINZ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand home prices fell sharply again in December from November while the number of sales rose, a key housing-sector report released Thursday showed. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA MAY MISS BOK'S 2% ECON GROWTH FORECAST - VICE FIN MIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in the South Korean economy this year may fall short of a Bank of Korea forecast for 2% expansion, Vice Minister of Strategy and Finance Bae Kook-hwan said Thursday. (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA DEC IMPORT PRICES -5.7% ON MO; DN FOR 2ND STRAIGHT MO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean import prices fell for a second straight month in December, Bank of Korea data showed Thursday, suggesting consumer inflation could ease in coming months. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: S KOREA COMMITTED TO ENSURING FOREIGN CURR LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean authorities reiterated Thursday that they will continue to ensure domestic banks have sufficient foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity, even as the dollar continued rising against the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOT: FUTURE RATE CUTS MAY NOT BE AS AGGRESSIVE AS RECENT CUTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday that potential future interest-rate cuts may not be as drastic as recent ones. (published 0532 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:MIER:MORE THAN 50% CHANCE OF RECESSION IN MALAYSIA 1H \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia has a more than 50% chance of slipping into recession in the first half of this year, an influential private think tank said Thursday. (published 0626 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ PM KEY: ECON TO "EFFECTIVELY STAND STILL" IN 2009 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister John Key said Thursday that New Zealand's economy would "effectively stand still" in 2009 due to deteriorating global economic conditions. (published 0454 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF CHINA VP:GOVTS' BAILOUTS NOT SOLVED ROOTS OF PROBLEM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of China Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Vice President Zhu Min said bailout plans by governments around the world to rescue markets haven't solved deep-rooted problems and warned of a "second round" of the financial crisis. (published 0233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI PM: MORE STIMULUS MEASURES FOR 2H BEING PLANNED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva said Thursday that the effect of the government's THB300 billion economic stimulus package will start to materialize in the first half of this year. (published 0421 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: THAI DECEMBER CONSUMER CONFIDENCE INDEX 74.8 VS 74.2 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer confidence in Thailand edged up in December, the first recovery after four straight months of declines, due to an improved political atmosphere in the country following the the formation of the new Democrat-led government. (published 0434 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI COMMERCE MIN: 2009 EXPORT-GROWTH TARGET HARD TO ACHIEVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's 3%-5% target range for export growth this year will be difficult to meet due to slowing global demand, but the government will do its best to achieve the target, Commerce Minister Pornthiva Nakasai said Thursday. (published 0615 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI OIL PLANS TO OFFER THB5B BONDS WITH 3YR-7YR MATURITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai Oil PCL\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday it plans to offer around THB5 billion of bonds with maturities of between three and seven years. (published 0357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:MALAYSIA 2009 BOND OFFERINGS TO TOTAL 27 VS 22 IN '08 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malayisan government will offer 27 bond issues this year, compared with 22 last year, to fund infrastructure spending, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0524 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: PHILIPPINE BKS' OUTSTANDING LOANS +22.9% ON YR IN NOV \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outstanding loans of Philippine banks grew at a slower pace in November than in the month before, in line with the global economic downturn. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(1/15) US STIMULUS NEARS $850B; PLAN FOCUSES ON JOB GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The price tag of the economic-recovery plan sought by President-elect Barack Obama has risen to nearly $850 billion, as congressional Democrats prepared to release a draft of the spending-and-tax-cut initiative as soon as Thursday. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =INTERVIEW: EX-MOF SAKAKIBARA: JAPAN MAY ACT IF DLR BELOW Y80 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar could fall below Y80.00 against the yen during the first half of this year, possibly prompting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary authorities to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the first time in nearly five years, a former top Japanese finance-ministry official said. (published 0622 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: CHINA TO CONTINUE TO OPT FOR YUAN STABILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese exports may be stumbling badly, but look for Beijing to limit the falls in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , opting for stability over a large-scale yuan decline. (published 2344 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: AUSTRALIAN GOVT SHOULD FAST TRACK TAX CUTS - ANALYSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's center-left Labor government should fast-track planned tax cuts to kickstart an economy facing a strong risk of following other world nations into a recession in 2009, analysts say. (published 0247 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW:SINGAPORE DEC EXPORTS LIKELY -20% LED BY ELECTRONICS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's key exports are expected to have contracted further in December as the global economic crisis continues to crimp demand for electronics and other goods from the city-state. (published 0458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(1/15) CREDIT MKTS: FED STRATEGY IS A BLAST FROM THE PAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve is considering purchases of longer-dated Treasurys as a way to assist the economy through lower rates. But, for now, it's likely to hold off on the move. (published 2357 GMT) [ 15-01-09 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090115e51f00081\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
