\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Euro Faces Another Leg Dn Vs Dlr On ECB Rate Buzz\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 1,005 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2009\par 20:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro is likely to face more selling pressure against the dollar next week as expectations of further interest-rate cuts from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained traction.\par \par \uc2 The ECB is widely forecast to cut its benchmark interest rates by 50 basis points at its rate-setting meeting Thursday to 2%. Speculation has increased that more cuts are likely in coming months as growth in the euro zone deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 That was quite a reversal of fortune for the euro. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied in the last month of 2008, hitting a 12-week high of $1.4720 against the dollar and a record high of GBP0.9804 versus sterling. The strength came partly from speculation that the ECB, which has already been behind the curve of rate-cutting among major central banks, may pause its easing campaign.\par \par \uc2 But market expectations have shifted gear in the new year as data from euro zone turned grimmer. This week's latest bout of data showed the inflation rate dropping below 2% in the euro zone, exports in Germany sharply contracting, and industrial productions in Germany and France crumbling.\par \par \uc2 Further rate cuts are expected to push down yields on euro-denominated financial assets and cut their yield advantage against dollar assets, damping global interest in buying the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The sentiment is that the ECB is required to play catchup in cutting interest rates," said Robert Blake, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Boston at State Street Global Markets LLC. "This could lead to further downward pressure on the euro for some time to come."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the euro-dollar is expected to trade between $1.32 and $1.36, while the dollar-yen will be between Y87 and Y92 next week.\par \par \uc2 In mid-afternoon trading Friday, the euro was at $1.3443 from $1.3723 late Thursday and the dollar was at Y90.39 from Y91.18, according to EBS. The euro was at Y121.50 from Y125.13, sterling was at $1.5166 from $1.5220, and the dollar was at CHF1.1164 from CHF1.0915.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, economic woes have also pinched the U.S., which lost 2.6 million jobs last year due to the financial crisis and economic recession. But the dire growth outlook in the U.S. has already been priced into the markets and the Federal Reserve has already cut interest rates to between 0% and 0.25%.\par \par \uc2 Derek Halpenny, European head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research in London at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2 , said that with expectations of further ECB rate cuts building, the euro is likely to unwind more of the gains made a month earlier. Within three months, he expected the euro to weaken to $1.27 versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar may also get a boost from risk aversion as bleak data from the U.S., euro zone and other countries raised concern that a recovery isn't in the cards soon.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists noted that after making some gains at the beginning of the week, global stock markets have failed to sustain the rally over the past few sessions while crude oil prices tumbled, a sign that investor appetite for risk may wane again.\par \par \uc2 A risk-averse environment tends to favor the dollar against the euro because the greenback is the world's No.1 reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , encouraging investors to seek comfort in super-safe U.S. government debt and short-term money assets.\par \par \uc2 The yen, also considered a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is another winner. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said that it will rise against the dollar, the euro, the pound and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the world if risk aversion puts more downward pressure on stocks in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Yen strength might be a main stream next week," said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank Ltd\uc2 . in New York. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may retest the Y87.13 level hit Dec. 17, the highest level against the dollar since 1995.\par \par \uc2 Continued strength in the yen may raise market concern that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains because a rising yen cuts the competitiveness of Japanese exports in the global markets. But Yanagihara said an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the BOJ is unlikely in the near term.\par \par \uc2 The pound is expected to get a new lease on life in the short term against the euro on the readjustments of ECB rate cut expectations. The pound has rebounded sharply against the euro from the record low of GBP0.9804 hit December 30.\par \par \uc2 Friday, the euro traded at GBP0.8872 from GBP0.9004 late Thursday, and touched an intraday low of GBP0.8842, its weakest level against sterling since mid-December.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said that the euro weakness is expected to persist in the near term, further dimming its move toward parity with the pound.\par \par \uc2 (Min Zeng writes about the Treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 201-938-2096; min.zeng@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.)\par \par \uc2 Corrected January 11, 2009 20:35 ET (01:35 GMT) [ 01-09-09 1454ET ]\par \par \uc2 The ECB is widely forecast to cut its benchmark interest rates by 50 basis points at its rate-setting meeting Thursday to 2%. Speculation has increased that more cuts are likely in coming months as growth in the euro zone deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 (In "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Euro Faces Another Leg Dn Vs Dlr On ECB Rate Buzz," at 1954 GMT on January 9, the ECB's benchmark rate was incorrect.) [ 01-11-09 1959ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020090109e5190003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
