\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Asian Summary\b0\par\par\uc2 1,799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2008\par 07:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] ASIAN SUMMARY: Holiday-thinned trade makes for some volatility in Asian stocks; Kospi down 3.0%, HSI down 2.8%, Taiwan off 2.9%, taking cue from Wall Street's losses; Shanghai down 2.9%, with banks leading way lower as concerns over economy outweigh positives from \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's rate cut yesterday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, JPY slips vs EUR, USD in quiet session with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  market shut; is some concern that Japanese authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY over next few weeks. USD/JPY last at 90.24 vs 90 late NY, EUR/JPY at 126.28 vs 125.56; EUR/USD at 1.3996 vs 1.3954. USD/KRW ends up at 1,338 vs 1,309 yesterday, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pulling pair off high of 1,349. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  lowers Ford credit ratings, citing increased risk it'll will have to undertake balance sheet restructuring which likely would involve loss for bondholders; little impact on Asian markets; \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut corporate family and probability of default ratings 2 notches to Caa3, lowers its speculative grade liquidity rating 1 notch to SGL-4. On data front, NZ's recession deepens, should keep RBNZ focussed on providing more interest rate relief; 3Q GDP negative for 3rd straight quarter, down 0.4% q/q, down 0.1% Y/Y. Singapore Nov inflation eases in line with expectations to 5.5% vs Oct's 6.4% as housing costs show signs of moderation, transport costs contract. February \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude down 39 cents at $39.52/bbl. Later, Taiwan export orders/industrial output 0800 GMT, HK BoPs 0830 GMT. Vietnam CPI due out. Europe has Italy retail sales, UK productivity while in U.S. there's final reading on 3Q GDP, Reuters/Uni of Michigan index, existing and new home sales, \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2  index.(CNG)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Indonesia shares down midday but off lows; main index last off 1.2% at 1329.309 vs down 1.3% earlier. Energy plays fall on oil price decline, although some now recovering on bargain buying. Gas provider \uc2 Perusahaan Gas Negara\uc2  (PGAS.JK) now steady at IDR1,800; Medco Energi (MEDC.JK) off 1.8% at IDR1,680; coal miner \uc2 Tambang Batubara Bukit Asam\uc2  (PTBA.JK) down 2.9%, Adaro (ADRO.JK) steady at IDR485. Banks, consumer-related shares still down: bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) off 1.4% at IDR6,950; heavyweight \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) down 7.2% at IDR10,300; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 2.4% at IDR2,000, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) at IDR660, down 4.4%. Non-energy resources firms gaining as seen as safe bet amid weakening in other sectors: nickel miner \uc2 Aneka Tambang\uc2  (ANTM.JK) +0.9% at IDR1,080, International Nickel (INCO.JK) +2.1% at IDR1,940. Support pegged for main index at 1320. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Easing Singapore inflation amid "dismal" exports and industrial output data puts pressure on MAS to shift undisclosed SGD NEER policy band downward prior to or at April meeting, says HSBC economist Prakriti Sofat; November CPI +5.5%, as forecast, vs +6.4% in October. "However, it is important to bear in mind that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment would do little in the face of the complete collapse in external demand which is impacting the whole region."(VIN)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan government bonds end tad higher, aided by 2.9% fall in stocks (after shedding 3.4% yesterday), and as traders await result of reopened 10-year bond auction, 2009's bond offering plan later today. Taiwan usually sells about NT$400 billion in government bonds each year, with about NT$100 billion - NT$110 billion expected in 1Q. "Even if it (the first quarter amount) is more than that, yields may rise slightly and fall later because players expect another rate cut by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the long-term yield trend is down," says local bank trader. Adds, the "bond plan is just noise because money is ample and short-term rates fell a lot." Yield on reopened 10-year bond ends down at 1.4269% vs last close at 1.4401% in when-issued trade; auction result tipped around 1.44%-1.47%.(ABP)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Key themes for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2009 will include deleveraging and a "gloomy race to the bottom in terms of economic growth," which should be USD positive in 1H09, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  ; but by 2H, USD should be hurt as current account deficit funded by rock-bottom interest rates takes toll. EUR, GBP should fall vs USD in 1H before climbing after that; Europe's emerging market exposure and slow-moving \uc2 ECB\uc2  argue for selling EUR/GBP on a probe to high 0.90s, targeting 0.80 in 2H, it says. For JPY it's "signs of 19989 all over again as Japanese investors threaten to cut foreign assets, driving dollar/yen below 80." In Asia, ex-Japan, KRW should rebound from "cellar dweller to top of the table"; CNY should be outperformer while trade-sensitive SGD, TWD and equity-sensitive INR face underlying tide of selling. Sluggish data, weak commodity prices, broad USD demand to make AUD/USD a sell above 0.70 in 1Q, en route to 0.60 and perhaps 0.55; 2H set to be more promising, but says buy AUD on the crosses.(RXM)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/INR extends gains, breaches 48.50, highest since Dec. 12 as local shares fall; pair was at 48.37 earlier, 48.01 last close, dealer with state-run bank tips resistance around 48.65 but expects pair to remain strong, boosted by month-end demand from oil companies. "There is also demand from foreign banks to capitalize on the higher rates in the offshore market," says dealer. 1-month NDF presently quoting at 49.07. Sensex down 1.7% at 9760.14. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0505 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Sensex down 1.7% at 9762.40, in line with regional markets on profit taking after recent rise. 28 index issues down, just 2 up; index tipped to find immediate support at 9700. Blue chips lead index fall; \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) down 1.5% at INR1,265, \uc2 ICICI Bank\uc2  (532174.BY) off 1.6% at INR439. "Weak global cues have led to soft opening. Heavyweights and banks which had outperformed lately are witnessing some amount of unwinding," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, index to remain volatile, with downward bias before derivatives expiry Wednesday, lower foreign participation due to year-end holidays, expects strong support at 9500. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) down 3.9% at INR180.70, Larsen (500510.BY) off 3.7% at INR784.85. HDFC (500010.BY) down 3.4% at INR1,443, Mahindra (500520.BY) down 4.3% at INR293.40. (GSV)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Singapore inflation eases in line with expectations to 5.5% in November vs 6.4% in October, as housing costs show signs of moderation, transport costs contract; markets unlikely to move much on data, with attention now shifting to measures government will take to pump-prime economy. Most analysts say January 22 budget will push for fiscal incentives that might keep prices elevated, but within government's 2009 inflation estimate of 1-2%.(PRV)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] U.S. New and Existing Home Sales will be biggest driver for Aussie bond futures offshore, AUD offshore, says ICAP Economist Adam Carr. Price moves could swing in large ranges. "Volumes are going to be incredibly light, so I imagine any price action we're going to get will be incredibly choppy." Adds traders reluctant to take on new positions; "everyone wants to close their book and go home." Says no clear target levels for AUD/USD, last at 0.6839, bond futures should retrace some of their earlier losses. March 3-year contract may test 96.58, now 96.575, 10-years may find resistance at 95.90, now 95.86. (EGC)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Listless AUD/USD may bounce if \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Trichet fires hawkish shots overnight and U.S. 3Q GDP is worse than expected, says Barclays Capital Strategist David Forrester. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be tuned to Trichet to see if he is any more dovish, or for comments on recent EUR strengthening when he speaks at a conference in Paris. "The European economy is struggling, a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help the economy; we don't know what officials think about the strength of the euro." U.S. GDP also in focus, but any AUD/USD gains may be capped. "The Aussie dollar is really a U.S. dollar story." Expects resistance to kick on soft commodity prices. Last at 0.6844. (EGC)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] HSI down 2.8% at 14,210.68 midday, taking Wall Street cues, with declines led by profit-taking in various sectors, including HK properties and commodities. Louis Tse at Value Convergence CEF says China rate cut was highly expected, so investors are selling on news to take profits. Adds, HSI could manage to stay above 14,000 level ahead of Christmas break, if \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) doesn't test psychologically key HK$70.00 support for now. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  down 1.1% at HK$73.25. Among HK properties, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) down 6.2% at HK$72.30, Henderson Land (0012.HK) down 6.1% at HK$29.85, after rising 14.9%, 31.4% over past month. Among commodities, Cnooc (0883.HK) down 4.4% at HK$6.69, Shenhua (1088.HK) down 5.8% at HK$15.40. Volume remains thin at HK$17.49 billion. (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Kospi falls further, last down 2.9% at 1145.93 in very thin volume as news of plans to force restructuring at construction companies, smaller shipbuilders that may become insolvent, sluggish U.S. stocks gives excuse for investors to take profits, say analysts. Also, "it was about time the Kospi underwent a correction after its faster recovery than global peers," says Lee Jae-mahn at \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  . "There's no momentum to help the Kospi reduce losses," says Lee; expects Kospi to remain weak rest of today. Bank shares, builders, shipbuilders extend losses on restructuring news; Shinhan Financial (055550.SE) down 4.0% at KRW30,200, \uc2 Daewoo Engineering\uc2  (047040.SE) off 6.5% at KRW9,240, \uc2 Samsung Heavy\uc2  (010140.SE) down 4.7% at KRW25,600. Techs, autos further down with Hynix (000660.SE) off 9.9% at KRW7,400, \uc2 Hyundai\uc2  motor (005380.SE) down 8.7% at KRW41,750. (SKS)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 23, 2008 01:26 ET (06:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090818e4cn0n5be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
