\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie in tight range against US$, firm against yen\b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2008\par 02:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 _________AUSTRALIAN CREDIT/\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (0115 GMT) SNAPSHOT__________\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (against previous Sydney close) <AUD=>  \par 0.6835/37  (0.6888/93)  <AUDNZD=R>1.1813/37 (1.1834/55)\par <AUDJPY=R> 61.43/48 (61.71/76)        <JPY=>89.88/92 (89.64/66)\par <AUDEUR=R> 0.4876/78(0.4819/24)      <EUR=>1.4015/18\par (1.4282/88) DEBT FUTURES                             CASH\par YIELDS 90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (MAR) 96.900( 0.000)  <AU3MBB=RR>\par 4.25(4.33) 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1> (MAR) 96.625(-0.045)  <AU3YT=RR> \par 3.14(3.09) 10-YR BOND <YTCc1> (MAR) 95.890(-0.755) \par <AU10YT=RR>\par  4.09(4.02) 3/10 SPREAD +0.705 (+0.735)    AUST/US 10-YR SPREAD\par  +196(+193) S&P/ASX 200 3550.2 (3588.5)    US10-YR <US10YT=RR> \par  2.14(2.09) ___________________________Dec\par 22_______________________________\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  DATA: Sales of new motor vehicles in Australia fell 5.2 percent in November, data showed on Monday, the fifth straight month of falls and further confirmation of the weakness of consumption. ---------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * The Aussie dollar <AUD=> was wedged in a tight trading range against the U.S. dollar on Monday with all the focus on the yen following dire Japanese data and talk the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports fell by a record 26.7 percent from a year earlier, data showed on Monday, as the global economic downturn hammered demand for Japanese goods.\par \par \uc2  * Japanese manufacturers' business sentiment hit a historic low in December and logged the sharpest month-on-month drop on record, battered by the global economic recession and a stronger yen.\par \par \uc2  * All of which helped nudge the Aussie up to 61.43 yen <AUDJPY=R>, from an early low of 60.76. The euro was the biggest gainer, climbing over a yen to to 125.68 <EURJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2  * Aussie marking time on the U.S. dollar around $0.6830, after last week's volatile action saw it jump from 66 cents to $0.7040 before easing back.\par \par \uc2  * Market awaiting more U.S. economic data this week, including new home sales, the latest estimate of Q3 GDP, house prices and personal income and consumption [ECI/US].\par \par \uc2  * Bond futures fell after their recent rallies, and in line with a pullback in U.S. Treasuries. Three-year futures <YTTc1> lost 0.045 points to 96.625 points, while the 10-year contract <YTCc1> fell 0.075 points to 95.890.\par \par \uc2  * But expectations Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may further cut interest rates next year to shield the economy from a global downturn should support bonds going forward.\par \par \uc2  * Bill futures <YBAc1> show markets are expecting interest rates to fall by at least 100 basis points by the end of March. Australia's benchmark cash rate stands at 4.25 percent now.\par \par \uc2  * Oil rose more than $1 to above $43 a barrel on Monday, buoyed by a weaker U.S. dollar and hopes that OPEC members will comply with the latest supply cuts.\par \par \uc2   (Reporting by Koh Gui Qing)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-BONDS/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -NOON|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081225e4cm00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
