\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall; Toyota Warns On Loss\b0\par\par\uc2 2,184 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2008\par 23:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's markets, banks, businesses and government offices closed for Emperor's birthday. \par  \par DJIA         8519.69  -59.42  -0.69% \par Nasdaq       1532.35  -31.97  -2.04% \par S&P 500       871.63  -16.25  -1.83% \par FTSE 100     4249.16  -37.77  -0.88% \par Xetra DAX    4639.02  -57.68  -1.23% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 26/32 -06/32 0.82% +9.2 \par 5Y Tsy   102 25/32 -10/32 1.42% +6.6 \par 10Y Tsy  114 03/32 -09/32 2.16% +3.0 \par  \par USD/JPY     90.00-05     +0.98% \par Range       90.47 -89.14 \par EUR/USD     1.3954-59    +0.20% \par Range       1.4123-1.3902 \par AUD/USD     0.6836-40    +0.13% \par Range       0.6892-0.6802 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   18.00/21.00% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   23.12/24.88% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   27.81/30.31% \par  \par Comex Gold          $847.20  +$9.80 \par Comex Gold Range    $836.70-$853 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $39.91   -$2.45 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell overnight as another drop in oil prices and a warning from \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  underscored the unsparing nature of the economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . yesterday forecast an operating loss for the current year, the first in the automaker's history since the Second World War, blaming a slump in the global automobile market and a sharp appreciation in the Japanese yen against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Toyota forecast a consolidated operating loss of 150 billion yen ($1.7 billion), for the fiscal year ending March 31, compared to a 2.27 trillion yen profit a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 "It's a kind of emergency that we've never experienced before," Toyota President Katsuaki Watanabe told reporters in Nagoya yesterday. "It's not yet possible to tell where the market's bottom will be."\par \par \uc2 The Japanese giant was thought to have developed a watertight strategy that would yield profits through thick and thin, making it the subject of managerial guides like the 2004 book "The Toyota Way." But the spreading recession caught up on Toyota, too, and it blamed a slump in the global automobile market and a sharp appreciation in the Japanese yen against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a likely loss.\par \par \uc2 American depositary shares of Toyota fell 5.45% to $60.88.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  was by far the weakest stock on the Dow Jones Industrial Average, falling 22% to $3.52. Analysts warned that the government rescue measure may not be enough to keep the car and truck maker out of bankruptcy court. Meanwhile, GM is gearing up for negotiations with a major union, which objected to the terms of the rescue. Some pundits warn that the bankruptcy of a major American manufacturer could push a recessionary unemployment situation into something akin to a depression.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  declined 12% to $2.59.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the Dow fell 59.42 points, dampening hopes for a "Santa Claus rally." Still, the Dow recovered from its worst levels of the day, when it was off more than 200 points, as defensive stocks like drug maker Merck and fast-food restaurant \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  offset weakness in consumer, financial and commodity stocks.\par \par \uc2 One hedge fund manager, who didn't wish to be identified because of his own clients' nerves, said the Madoff scandal has prompted investors to withdraw money from even the best-performing hedge funds. With fund of funds Fairfield Greenwich ensnared in the scandal, investors will likely be particularly wary of this kind of firm, which were supposed to perform due diligence when investing in funds for them, the hedge fund manager said.\par \par \uc2 "So funds of funds might have redemptions from investors, and also some money returned by hedge funds that don't want them as clients," he said.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, analysts said the Hang Seng Index may trend slightly higher before the end of the year on window dressing.\par \par \uc2 "The chances are still there for the Hang Seng Index to test its key (psychological) 16,000 point level in the near term, as underlying sentiment remains strong on window-dressing activities ahead of the year-end," said Steven Leung, a director at UOB KayHian Securities.\par \par \uc2 KGI Asia Ltd\uc2 .'s Chief Operating Officer Ben Kwong, however, warned investors to exercise caution, as any rebound may only be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 "My best-case scenario for the index target in December is 16,600. I'd advise investors to take some profit this week," he said, as he expects the weakness of global bourses and further evidence of a regional economic slowdown to hinder any further rises in Hong Kong early next year.\par \par \uc2 The softer tone in the U.S. dollar and a continued bounce from a Fibonacci support level enabled gold futures to finish higher overnight, traders and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures ended lower yesterday, weighed down by ongoing demand concerns amid thinner trading volume. Market participants were also keeping their powder dry ahead of Wednesday's release of the weekly U.S. oil inventory data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The onset of holiday trading conditions yesterday limited the dollar's net movement against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as it continued to be prone to large and volatile intraday swings versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 A shortened trading week featuring the close of many world financial centers for Christmas and Boxing Day on Thursday and Friday, respectively, has already significantly thinned trading desks and the ranks of market participants, leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally susceptible to exaggerated movements, but making it difficult to reliably discern trends.\par \par \uc2 Volatility did remain the rule of thumb for the dollar- euro pairing, as the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially regained the $1.4100 handle versus the dollar on the back of improved global risk appetites, only to settle back closer to Friday's closing levels.\par \par \uc2 Late in the session, the euro had improved to $1.3954 from $1.3988 earlier and from $1.3879 late Friday. Against the yen, the dollar was at Y90.00, up from Y89.84 earlier and Y89.24 late Friday. The euro strengthened to Y125.56 from Y123.97 on Friday, while the pound moved up to $1.4850 from $1.4788, and the dollar fetched 1.0919 Swiss francs, down from CHF1.1075 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 For its part, the yen was weaker on the latest release of dispiriting Japanese economic data, as Japan's November trade balance showed exports plunging 26.7% for the largest year-over-year drop since the data series began in January 1980.\par \par \uc2 There has also been renewed market wariness about yen strength since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise last week through the Y90.00 mark versus the dollar. That has prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry to warn of possible market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool the yen's recent ascent to 13-year highs against the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at CIBC World Markets said yesterday that given the incidence of weak Japanese data and the likelihood of a dismal fourth quarter for Japan's economy and exporters, Japan has "ample reason to stand in the way of a further yen rally."\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's \uc2 Bank of China\uc2 , also delivered its fourth interest-rate cut since September yesterday, amid reports that the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves may have fallen for the first time since December 2003.\par \par \uc2 A speech by a mid-level Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulatory official posted over the weekend on a Chinese Web site indicated some unspecified diminishment of China's reserves, which are the world's largest at $1.9 trillion as of the end of September.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut loan and deposit rates by 0.27 percentage points, effective today, in the Chinese government's latest move to stimulate the economy. It was the fifth time the bank has cut rates since September this year. The benchmark one-year yuan lending rate will fall to 5.31 percent from 5.58 percent and the one-year yuan deposit rate will fall to 2.25 percent from 2.52 percent.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 Treasurys bowed to supply pressure in a thin trade overnight but recouped some losses after the U.S. government auctioned a two-year note yielding below 1% for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The selling in Treasurys started the previous session and prices hit session lows right before the auction. Bonds moved off the lows after the record $38 billion two-year note auction. The Treasury Department is scheduled to sell a record $28 billion of five-year notes Tuesday afternoon U.S.-time\par \par \uc2 Reduced liquidity ahead of the Christmas holiday Thursday exaggerated bond price moves, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "People were worried about the auction given its size and thin market conditions. When that went OK, the market did better," said Carl Lantz, an interest-rate strategist in New York at \uc2 Credit Suisse Securities USA LLC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lantz added that Treasurys also got some support from the down trade in stocks and weakness in shares of life insurers tied to concerns about commercial real estate. As the value of property has fallen and lending conditions have tightened, concern has risen that property developers may find it hard to refinance the loans next year.\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trading in the U.S., the two-year note was down 6/32 to 100 26/32 to yield 0.82%. The five-year note was down 10/32 to 102 25/32 to yield 1.42%. The 10-year note was down 9/32 to 114 3/32 to yield 2.16%, and the 30-year bond was down 1 12/32 to 138 16/32 to yield 2.62%. The bond equivalent yield on three-month T-bill traded at 0.02%.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark yield curve, the yield spread between the two- and 10-year notes, flattened to 134 basis points from 139 basis points Friday.\par \par \uc2 The low yield from the two-year note sale is a boon for the government as it can still borrow cheaply from investors to finance a ballooning budget shortfall. It also suggested that investors are still willing to park cash in highly liquid paper at year end and stay away from risky bets. [ 22-12-08 2214GMT ]\par \par \uc2 The key test for Treasurys will come in early January. Some investors may start to defect to other high-quality assets since there is almost no juice left in a two-year note following a steep rally this year that sent yields down to record lows last week. Many investors already moved out of the two-year sector and into 10- and 30-year maturities to squeeze some extra yields over the past couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 "The auction went OK. For now, people are still nervous and they still want to hide in Treasurys," said E. Craig Coats Jr., head of fixed income in New York at Keefe, Bruyette & Woods Inc., who began trading bonds in 1969 and was co-head of fixed income at Salomon Brothers in the 1980s, when it was the world's biggest bond trader.\par \par \uc2 But Coats said he wouldn't buy Treasurys because of the low yields. Leading into January, if credit markets improve, he said investment-grade corporate bonds are a good alternative to Treasurys. However, if economic data deteriorate further, the two-year note's yield may retest the record low again, he said.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government bond market, meanwhile, is expected to be quiet this week as local and overseas players alike take a break over the winter holiday season, traders in Tokyo said. Even key data due out later this week on November industrial production and the consumer price index are unlikely to spark major movement in the JGB market, they said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Treasury Awards $38B In 2-Year Notes \par Chicago Fed Data Show Deeper Recession \par GM CEO Defends UAW, Hopeful For A Deal \par S&P Cuts Ratings Again On Avis, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dollar Thrifty\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Court OKs Lehman Deal To Sell Neuberger Berman \par CFTC OKs International Derivatives Clearinghouse \par Preliminary Deadline For Ecuador CDS Auction Expires \par Weather Chills Weekend Holiday Shopping \par South Korea To Ease Foreign Acct Rules To Help Cos - Reuters \par BOJ Gov:Japan Econ Conditions May Get More Severe For A While \par Security Council OKs Dec 31 End Of Intl Force Mandate In Iraq \par NZ 3Q Adjusted GDP -0.4% Vs 2Q; Market Expected -0.5% \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2145  New Zealand  3Q GDP \par 0500  US           Dec 20  Johnson Redbook Index \par 0930  UK           3Q Productivity \par 0930  UK           Oct UK service sector figures \par 0930  UK           3Q UK economic growth stats \par 0930  UK           Nov Major British Banking Groups stats \par 1245  US           Dec 20  ICSC/\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales \par 1330  US           3Q GDP, final \par 1455  US           Dec Reuters/University of Michigan Survey, final \par 1500  US           Nov Existing Home Sales \par 1500  US           Dec Richmond Fed Mfg Index \par 1500  US           Nov New Home Sales \par 2200  US           Dec 20  ABC/Washington Post C