\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan steps up warning as yen hits 13-yr high vs dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto   \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2008\par 04:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise after it hit a 13-year high against the dollar, adding to pressure on the Bank of Japan to cut interest rates to protect an export-dependent economy already in recession.\par \par \uc2 Market players said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were still only trying to talk down the yen for now, but a further jump in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any subsequent fall in Tokyo shares could prompt them to act.\par \par \uc2 The dollar sank near 87 yen on Wednesday in the wake of the Federal Reserve's hefty interest rate cut, which had led more analysts to forecast that the BOJ will cut rates on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "We have conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, and we'll take appropriate measures, which includes that (option)," Japan's top government spokesman Takeo Kawamura told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "As U.S. interest rates approach zero, the dollar's weakness is continuing, and it's solely the dollar that is weakening... We must closely watch it given its impact on the real economy," the chief cabinet secretary added.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has stayed out of the market since a yen-selling spree totalling 35 trillion yen ($398.9 billion) in 2003 and the first quarter of 2004, when the government waged a campaign to prevent a rapid yen rise from derailing a then-fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Naoyuki Shinohara, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pointman, also voiced a slightly tougher line than usual on Thursday, telling reporters that the ministry would take proper steps as needed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The ministry's standard line has been that it is watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets carefully.\par \par \uc2 "The tone is getting stronger, and the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would rise if the dollar falls sharply below 87 yen overnight," said Masafumi Yamamoto, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Japan for the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MOF holds jurisdiction over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Japan, like the United States, is already in recession, with major companies such as carmakers Toyota and Honda slashing output as customers around the world close their wallets.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting the gloomy outlook, the government is expected to cut its assessment of the economy in December for the third month running to warn of a worsening business climate, the Nikkei newspaper reported.\par \par \uc2 As troubles deepen, ruling party lawmakers on Thursday agreed to put forward a bill in next year's parliament that would enable a government body to resume buying shares from banks -- a measure adopted amid a Japanese banking crisis in the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 They seek to allow the Banks' Shareholdings Purchase Corp, which the government set up in 2002 when Japanese banks were burdened with bad loans to companies, to buy up to 20 trillion yen of shares. That would be much larger than a total of about 1.6 trillion yen worth of shares it bought from 2002 to 2006.\par \par \uc2 EYES ON BOJ\par \par \uc2 The yen has risen more than 20 percent against the dollar this year due to risk aversion among investors who have unwound carry trade, whereby they had borrowed low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as Japanese yen to invest in higher-yielding assets abroad, amid a deepening U.S. slowdown and a global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded around 88 yen on Thursday, still within sight of Wednesday's 13-year low of 87.13 yen.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's dramatic rate cut has raised the pressure on a reluctant BOJ to follow suit at its two-day policy review ending on Friday. U.S. interest rates are now below Japanese rates for the first time since February 1993.\par \par \uc2 Two-thirds of economists expect the BOJ to cut rates to 0.10 percent from the current policy target of 0.30 percent, a Reuters poll showed, although \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have been cautious about cutting Japan's already low rates.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government officials have called on the BOJ to take more action to help the economy and ease corporate funding strains, and the finance minister kept up the pressure. (Additional reporting by Leika Kihara, Yuzo Saeki, Hideyuki Sano and Chisa Fujioka; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/JAPAN (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081218e4ci0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
