\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie at 2-mth high vs dollar, risks linger\b0\par\par\uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2008\par 23:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Aussie at two-month high vs U.S. dollar; steady vs yen\par \par \uc2 * Fed's cut boosts appetite for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Worries about the global economy however limit gains\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Dec 18 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar jumped to two-month highs against the weak U.S. dollar on Thursday, as investors still flocked to higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Federal Reserve's decision to cut interest rates to near zero.\par \par \uc2 The yield gap between the 10-year U.S. and Australian government bonds widened to 187 basis points from 166 points a week earlier. Australia's cash rate is at 4.25 percent, although investors expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slash it to 3 percent by March next year.\par \par \uc2 "The search for yields is always there, but with uncertainty about the global economy mounting, I can't see the Aussie holding above 70 U.S. cents for long," said Joseph Capurso, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commonwealth Bank.\par \par \uc2 "Commodity prices are still depressed and the world economy is likely to get weaker and these will work against the Aussie. Also, I don't expect people betting big ahead of the year end."\par \par \uc2 By 4:15 p.m. (0515 GMT), the Aussie <AUD=D2> was trading at $0.7046, having risen to an intra-day high of $0.7080, its strongest level since Oct. 14. It has now gained more than 4 cents this week as the U.S. dollar continued to fall broadly.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie's rise came despite the International Monetary Fund likely to cut its 2009 world economic growth forecast again next month. Last month, the Fund sharply lowered its forecast for world growth next year to 2.2 percent, down 0.8 percentage point from an October forecast [ID:nN17269049].\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were steady around $40 a barrel while base metals were shaky as investors still cut holdings on worries a global recession would cut demand for natural resources. Australia is a big exporter of commodities.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie <AUDJPY=R> was broadly steady against the yen, holding around 61.70 yen, with lower stock markets keeping gains muted. The yen traded near a 13-year high on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with gains capped by worries of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [nT205941]\par \par \uc2 All eyes are on the BOJ which started its two-day meeting on Thursday. Two-thirds of economists polled by Reuters expect it to cut interest rates this week. [ID:nT238594]\par \par \uc2 A rate cut might temporarily spur carry trades, where investors buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the cheaper ones such as the yen.\par \par \uc2 Australian bond futures were mixed, with the long end supported by growing demand from investors seeking higher yields after the Fed chopped short-term rates to near zero. Australian three-year futures <YTTc1> shed 0.035 points to 96.585 while the 10-year contract <YTCc1> gained 0.065 points to 95.915. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------(Snapshot at 4:20 p.m./0520\par GMT)----------------- FUTURES                                  \par CASH YIELD 90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (MAR) 96.880(-0.010)  <AU3MBB=RR>\par 4.38 (4.38) 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1> (MAR) 96.585(-0.035)  <AU3YT=RR>\par  3.18 (3.17) 10-YR BOND <YTCc1> (MAR) 95.915(+0.065) \par <AU10YT=RR> 4.08 (4.16) AUD/USD <AUD=> 0.7040 (0.6985) US 10-YR\par  <US10YT=RR> 2.18 (2.28)\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par  AUD VS   2-YR          10-YR            *A