\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Recoups Post-Fed Losses; Down Vs Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2008\par 07:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds yen's rise against the dollar, latest Nakagawa remark and more trader comments.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Monahan and Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar in Asia on Wednesday recovered some of the ground it lost overnight following the Fed's rate cut to as low as zero percent, but traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still fall further due to bearishness toward the American economy's outlook.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in late Asian trading, the dollar dropped to as low as Y88.24 against the yen, from an intraday high of Y89.22 earlier in the day, on expectations of selling by European players once the day starts there.\par \par \uc2 The dollar tumbled overnight after the Federal Open Market Committee slashed the Fed funds target rate to a range of 0% to 0.25% and signaled that quantitative easing would become its primary policy tool.\par \par \uc2 Initially Wednesday, profit-taking helped push the dollar back up to Y89.22 compared with an intraday low of Y88.63 Tuesday, and to $1.3998 to the euro from $1.4150 a day earlier. It also gained to $1.5573 against the sterling compared with $1.5643 late in New York.\par \par \uc2 It dropped against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, as Wednesday was the first day for Asian traders to react to the Fed cut.\par \par \uc2 Dollar-buying against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon petered out because of the overwhelmingly pessimistic outlook toward the U.S. economy, in spite of the Fed's de facto launching of a zero-interest rate policy.\par \par \uc2 "While the Fed's move was decisive and commendable, it also showed how severe the conditions are that the U.S. economy is going through," said Minoru Shioiri, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities. "The dollar is a 'sell' against almost all the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa reportedly saying that Tokyo isn't thinking now of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen, according to The Nikkei, weighed on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside against the yen. Later though, Nakagawa said that Japan is ready to take needed steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help the economy, a clear warning that Tokyo might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen.\par \par \uc2 In spite of his latest remarks, traders don't think that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, because the dollar isn't weakening just against the yen but across the board, so Tokyo action to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now wouldn't be effective.\par \par \uc2 "It is very doubtful that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective in pushing the yen down," said Yuji Saito, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Group at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "And because other Asian nations have been suffering from rises in the dollar against their local units in recent weeks, it's difficult for Japan to buy the dollar."\par \par \uc2 As such, many traders said the dollar could fall below the recent low of Y88.10 and test support at Y85 in the coming weeks. The yen has not traded under Y85 since July 1995.\par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Yoshida, a senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said there is a good amount of automated dollar-selling orders below Y88.00, meaning its decline after breaking below that mark could be rapid.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said that the Bank of Japan could very well follow the Fed's lead and lower rates at the end of its two-day meeting Friday. But even if the BOJ cuts rates to 0.1% or so from the current 0.3%, that might not weaken the yen, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "This would not be a yen downside, because the underlying rate differentials are already so small," said Tohru Sasaki, \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, "many players are expecting the BOJ will cut its interest rate. So if it doesn't, the yen is likely to strengthen and may race to the next psychologically-important level of Y85.00," Yoshida said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also dropped broadly against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Against the Thai baht, for example, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a seven-week low of THB34.63, in spite of suspected mild \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Thai authorities to slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. The dollar was at THB34.78-34.79 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar/baht is following the softer trend of the dollar/regionals. Compared with regional peers, there's still more room for the pair to continue heading down," said a Bangkok-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also plunged to 32.686 against the Taiwanese dollar, its lowest level since Nov. 5, and to 46.93 against the Philippine peso, its lowest level since Oct. 15.\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained against its U.S. counterpart too, but traders said the 'aussie' would have trouble rising above key resistance at 0.7000 because commodity prices remain weak and the slowing global growth story dominates.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of wood to chop at that 0.7000 level," said \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Jonathan Cavenagh.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              88.99-01   88.97-02   +0.02  89.21   88.69   -20.15 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.4086-90  1.4036-40  +0.36  1.4090  1.3998   -3.51 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.5617-22  1.5572-76  +0.29  1.5625  1.5562  -21.40 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1177-81  1.1240-44  -0.56  1.1253  1.1174   -1.36 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.2012-18  1.2054-60  -0.35  1.2070  1.2000  +19.37 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.6961-66  0.6954-59  +0.10  0.6970  0.6913  -20.58 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.5794-00  0.5777-87  +0.29  0.5806  0.5778  -24.39 \par EUR/JPY Yen              125.34-39  124.91-96  +0.34  125.42  124.62  -22.88 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki and Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2937; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com [ 17-12-08 0630GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081217e4ch0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
