\par\par \b \uc2 DJ BOJ Shirakawa: Japan Econ May Turn Negative In FY09 - FT\b0\par\par\uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2008\par 02:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's economy is likely to contract in the year ending March 2010, Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa told the Financial Times, noting that the bank was poised next month to reverse its forecast of a mild recovery.\par \par \uc2 His forecast that "the economy for fiscal 2009 may turn negative" a downgrade from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current estimate of 0.6% growth underscores widespread pessimism about the prospects for a quick recovery of the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The revelation of the bank's concern about what Shirakawa called "enormous" risk factors to the economy comes amid government efforts to buoy the economy, which shrank in the second and third quarters, that include emergency measures announced on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In his first interview with a foreign newspaper since assuming the governorship in April, Mr Shirakawa was relatively relaxed about the looming prospect of a return of deflation, expected next year. He stressed instead the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's focus on preserving the stability of the financial system, repeating a view that a mild fall in consumer prices had accompanied Japan's six-year growth spurt this decade.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa stressed that ultra-loose monetary policy in the years to 2006, when interest rates were zero, had helped stabilise the banking system but had done little to stimulate growth, the FT reported.\par \par \uc2 On the possible return to headline deflation, he said: "What is important is the expected inflation rate of the medium to long term." Rising consumer prices caused by steeper commodity costs had not fed through into higher inflation expectations, he said, suggesting the reverse might be true as consumer prices fell.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa declined to discuss speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the yen's appreciation below Y90 to the dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Stressing that any decision about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be made by the finance ministry, he did acknowledge that a recent Group of Seven statement appeared to give \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  the green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, he said: "At the end of the day, the exchange rate is determined by market forces."\par \par \uc2 Click here to go to Dow Jones NewsPlus, a web front page of today's most important business and market news, analysis and commentary: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnewsplus.com/access/al?rnd=yM6Kqy729UXoSd7vO3GJww%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnewsplus.com/access/al?rnd=yM6Kqy729UXoSd7vO3GJww%3D%3D}}}\uc2 . You can use this link on the day this article is published and the following day.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020081215e4cf000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
