\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ gov:Japan economy may shrink in FY 2009/10-FT\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2008\par 00:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the financial crisis click [ID:nCRISIS]) (Adds more comments, background)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 15 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan governor said the nation's economy may contract in the fiscal year starting next April 1 and that markets, not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , determine \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, the Financial Times reported on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The newspaper report came before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's closely watched tankan corporate survey, which showed Japanese business sentiment at a nearly seven-year low, adding gloom to the outlook for an economy already in recession. [ID:nT91198]\par \par \uc2 "Until recently, the Japanese economy was relatively immune from turbulence in credit markets overseas. But the picture changed after the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ," BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa told the newspaper in an interview.\par \par \uc2 He said global economic conditions had changed significantly since the BOJ's latest twice-yearly economic report, in which it said Japan's economy would pick up gradually some time after the middle of next fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 "But since then, the global economy has changed a lot. I think the economy for fiscal year 2009 may turn negative."\par \par \uc2 In its outlook report released in late October, the BOJ's policy board produced a median forecast of 0.6 percent growth for fiscal 2009/10. [ID:nTKZ003041]\par \par \uc2 "This recession is a synchronised recession so I don't think the trajectory of the Japanese economy is much different from the rest of the world," Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar tumbled to 88.10 yen <JPY=> on Friday, its lowest since mid-1995, prompting some speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the yen's rise, which could hurt Japan's export-led economy further. [FRX/]\par \par \uc2 Asked if Tokyo should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a Group of Seven (G7) statement in October expressing worries about excessive volatility in the yen was interpreted as giving Tokyo the green light to step into the market, Shirakawa declined to discuss it directly.\par \par \uc2 Repeating the G7 stance to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closely and cooperate as needed, Shirakawa said that "monetary policy does not just focus on exchange rates", noting that the finance ministry, not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , decides whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It is not appropriate to frame the discussion as if we can manipulate or control the level of the exchange rate. Of course, monetary policy affects exchange rates indirectly, but there is no one-to-one correspondence."\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, the exchange rate is determined by market forces."\par \par \uc2 On the possibility of a return to deflation in Japan, Shirakawa said: "What is important is the expected inflation rate in the medium to long term."\par \par \uc2 He said the consumer inflation rate would decline rapidly from now on, but the BOJ was "not observing a change in the long-run expected inflation rate", the FT reported. (Reporting by Yoko Nishikawa; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/JAPAN-SHIRAKAWA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081214e4ce000m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
