\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,008 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2008\par 09:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ASO TELLS MTG ABOUT PLAN FOR EXTRA STIMULUS MEASURES - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Taro Aso on Friday told a meeting of government and ruling coalition officials that he is planning to implement additional economic stimulus measures centering on measures to address a deteriorating job market and credit crunch, Kyodo News reported Friday. (published 0554 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =NAKAGAWA: WANT TO FINALIZE NEW ECON PACKAGE QUICKLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa said Friday that the government wants to quickly finalize an additional economic stimulus package, suggesting it may be announced as early as late Friday. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NAKAGAWA: NOT MULLING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOR NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa rejected the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for now, only saying the government is keeping an eye on market movement. (published 0656 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =JAPAN MOF WARNS ABOUT SHARP YEN RISE; ACTION HINTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Top Japanese finance ministry officials Friday expressed concern about the sharp rise in the yen, with one official warning that Tokyo is ready to take "appropriate action" if needed in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 0611 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF FRANCE CUTS 4Q GDP FORECAST TO -0.7% QQ FROM -0.5QQ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of France revised down its gross domestic product forecast for the fourth quarter to a contraction of 0.7% compared with the third quarter, according to its monthly report published Friday. (published 0731 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ITALIAN LABOR UNION STRIKES OVER BERLUSCONI'S ECONOMIC POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's largest labor union CGIL is holding a strike Friday to protest against the government's handling of the economic recession. (published 0740 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET MIN: GERMANY HAS MORE ROOM FOR ECON STIMULUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Eric Woerth said Friday that Germany should launch a larger economic stimulus plan because it has the resources for it. (published 0737 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN NOV CONSUMER SENTIMENT INDEX LOGS RECORD LOW OF 28.4 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's consumer sentiment index in November dropped to its lowest level since the government started the survey in 1982, mainly due to deteriorating employment conditions and the flagging global economy. (published 0614 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN RECESSION LIKELY TO GET WORSE -CABINET OFFICE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan will likely face a deep, protracted economic recession because faltering growth in the nation's trade partners could eventually lead to more layoffs and less bank lending at home, a Cabinet Office report said Friday. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL:1Q ECON GROWTH TO SLOW, MAY KEEP SLOWING AFTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic growth will slow in the first quarter and could keep slipping afterwards, a senior party official said during a local financial forum Friday. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MIN:NOVEMBER INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT GROWTH SLOWER VS OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's industrial production growth slowed in November from October, Minister of Industry and Information Technology Li Yizhong said Friday. (published 0352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CHINA NOV RETAIL SALES GROWTH EASES;SEEN FALLING MORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's retail sales stayed relatively resilient in November, but a continued easing in growth indicates consumption is slowing in the world's fourth-largest economy. (published 0440 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECON PLANNER: DOMESTIC SLOWDOWN IS "PROMINENT PROBLEM" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic planning agency said Friday the slowdown in China's economy has become a "prominent problem," and it will work to prevent large swings in the economy in 2009. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA, JAPAN, S KOREA TO HOLD RARE SUMMIT ON ECONOMIC CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FUKUOKA, Japan --The leaders of China, Japan and South Korea will hold a rare joint meeting Saturday as they try to work out how best to cushion Asia from the global economic crisis. (published 0250 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIA TO SPEED UP A$4.7B INFRA SPEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd unveiled a A$4.7 billion infrastructure spending package Friday designed to boost growth and create 32,000 jobs. (published 0514 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =KOREA EXPANDS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SWAP PACTS WITH JAPAN AND CHINA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea has signed bilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap agreements with China and Japan, moves that it hopes will keep the Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from weakening further and lighten the pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. (published 0639 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =BANK OF KOREA EXPECTS '09 GDP GROWTH WORST SINCE '98 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea expects the local economy to expand only 2.0% next year, its most sluggish pace of growth since 1998, and a sharp slowdown from this year's projected 3.7% expansion. (published 0141 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INDIA OCT INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT SHRINKS 0.4% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's industrial output contracted 0.4% from a year earlier in October, compared with an upwardly revised 5.45% rise in September, government data issued Friday show. (published 0641 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK TO LEND $14 BLN TO INDIA OVER NEXT 3 YRS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The World Bank said Friday it plans to lend $14 billion to India over the next three years to fund infrastructure projects. (published 0601 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: TO RISE FRIDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-end and long-end interest rate futures remain capped, but may well recover Friday. (published 0709 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =ASIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DLR/YEN HITS 13-YR LOW AS AUTO DEAL FAILS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Senate's failure to reach a deal to rescue U.S. carmakers sent the dollar plummeting to a 13-year low against the yen in Asia Friday, prompting Japanese authorities to fire warning shots into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. (published 0658 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: TANKAN LIKELY TO PUT THE SQUEEZE ON THE BOJ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A major survey due Monday of the Japanese corporate mood could prod the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into cutting interest rates closer to zero as soon as next Friday - for what it's worth, that is. (published 0024 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: RINGGIT TO FALL MORE AS INVESTORS FRET OVER TRADE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As export-oriented economies fall out of favor in global markets, the Malaysian ringgit is likely to be one of Asia's worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. dollar next year. (published 0642 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alexander Teddy, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9273; alexander.teddy@dowjones.com [ 12-12-08 0812GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081212e4cc000ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
