\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: False Hope Seen In US Strategists' Japan View\b0\par\par\uc2 By Riva Froymovich   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2008\par 17:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)-- Despite what a U.S.- or Europe-based investment analyst might say, it's unlikely that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is bullying local participants to help moderate the yen's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Many experienced analysts outside Japan are convinced that even though the MOF hasn't officially \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the past year, the Japanese government has been quietly been influencing the market one way or another. As a result, betting on a higher yen against the dollar has become a top trading recommendation.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists in Tokyo say this view of the MOF is out of touch. Representatives for the MOF in New York declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These contrasting views of the market stem from history, geography and a little bit of hope.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the MOF has been known to influence markets without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly. The government might lean on a Japanese pension fund to buy foreign assets to help maintain a cheap yen, for example.\par \par \uc2 In fact, portfolio flows last week recorded net outflows from Japan for the sixth consecutive week, according to Tohru Sasaki, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "We believe it is mainly from pension funds for rebalancing their portfolios," he said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors have been net buyers of foreign equities for 11 consecutive weeks, buying more than JPY3 trillion of foreign equities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts in the West also cite recently narrow, rangebound trading for the dollar versus the yen as another indication. While other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs have been subject to sharp moves, the dollar has remained on a short leash against the yen.\par \par \uc2 In addition, back in October, Japan Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa embarked on some verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a sign that the government was concerned with the rising value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japanese Analysts Have A Different Story \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, analysts on the ground in Japan have a different story. They say the MOF doesn't have the same influence or vigor that it once had and dismiss such talk as unlikely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The MOF had encouraged pension funds to invest more in foreign assets right after the MOF decided to stop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Tomoko Fujii, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think the MOF has the same energy as before," she said. "I don't think that really works anymore. It's an investor decision."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, Japan no longer has a massive trade surplus, which means it has smaller buying power abroad to affect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the same way as in the past, rendering direct invention less of a last-resort threat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fujii credited the low volatility in the dollar-versus-yen rate to declining interest rates. If both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are lower-yielding, there is no reason to expect a radical move as speculative buying has diminished, she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We continue to believe that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even if the dollar breaks Y90," agreed Sasaki of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials understand the yen's strength stems from market fundamentals and not speculation, he said, adding that the yen is merely back to an "equilibrium level from extremely weak levels."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is, however, a rationale behind U.S. and European traders betting on an active MOF - the hope for a stable yen, the last safe haven at this point.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.S. monetizing its debt and growing fears of a run on the dollar, or at least a significant decline, due to a fed funds rate near zero and additional quantitative easing, the dollar has lost some of its luster as the recognized safe asset. It also has lost a source of strength as volatility calms, slowing the unwinding of investments back into the greenback and encouraging investors to return to equities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shorting the dollar versus yen has become the safest and most popular bet among many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders. Investors have largely exited most positions in the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , except for the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In an environment where deleveraging flows have abated, the dollar appears increasingly vulnerable to very low yields and quantitative easing. At the same time, the euro's blemishes remain significant and make it an unappealing alternative to the dollar right now," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. "[We] continue to view the yen as a safer vehicle to play a bearish dollar view."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Riva Froymovich reports on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. The writer can be reached at 201-938-5063 or by email at riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments [ 12-11-08 1151ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020081211e4cb00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
