\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE: Asian Shares Slump On US Woes; Nikkei Falls 6.9%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2008\par 08:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets fell Thursday - some of them to multiyear lows - after a late-day slump on Wall Street, with the health of the banking and auto sectors still cause for much concern.\par \par \uc2 "We haven't seen bargain hunting because nobody knows whether anything is a bargain anymore - value is disappearing overnight," said Najeeb Jarhom, senior vice president of research at AmFraser in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Falls accelerated as the day wore on: Australia's S&P/ASX 200 ended down 4.2% at its lowest level since early 2004, with Japan's Nikkei shedding 6.9%, hurt in part by a surprise trade deficit in October; Korea's Kospi ended with a 6.7% decline with Taiwan's market down 4.5% at a more than six-year trough.\par \par \uc2 Margin lenders "are flat out with their margin calls," said Chris Blair, head of retail at \uc2 Patersons Securities\uc2  in Sydney; analysts elsewhere noted some redemption activity, though others said the continued slide in markets might prove attractive for braver, longer-term investors.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was recently down 6.2% with India's Sensex off 5.1%.\par \par \uc2 Light trading volume showed the level of caution among investors; "the market continues to trade on fear. Fundamentals are going to take a back seat for a while. It's like (watching) a terminal patient," said broker David Price of \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  in New Zealand.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen was notably higher with central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in several markets in Asia to keep a lid on the U.S. dollar; risk aversion was showing up in wider credit spreads while crude oil futures came in for some selling on demand-related concerns.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures were down 1%-1.5% in screen trade and European spreadbetting firms were calling stocks there to fall; CMC Markets tipped London's FTSE-100 to drop 93 points.\par \par \uc2 Asia's declines came after the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed under 8000 for the first time in more than five-and-a-half years as investors worried about the future of U.S. auto makers and banks; it declined 5.1% while the Nasdaq Composite dropped 6.5%.\par \par \uc2 There were ongoing concerns about the U.S. economy with poor data Wednesday on the housing market and gloomy minutes from the recent meeting of the Federal Open Markets Committee.\par \par \uc2 Some were starting to talk about the risk of a global deflationary spiral: "Faced with this prospect, central banks (in Asia, ex Japan) are loosening monetary conditions and will continue to do so well into 2009," said analysts at Standard Chartered.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks suffered with the Semiconductor Industry Association forecasting global chip sales for 2009 to decline 5.6%; Hynix Semi shed 15% in Seoul with Powerchip Semi down 6.9% in Taiwan and \uc2 Elpida Memory\uc2  off 3.7% in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "As dramatic declines in consumer and industrial confidence began developing in late summer, order cancellations began to grow and in many cases, slowing orders degenerated into a complete stop in orders as players across the supply chain moved to extremely cautious positions," said iSuppli senior vice president, market intelligence services, Dale Ford.\par \par \uc2 Shipping stocks were hit with Divay Goel, director and head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Drewry Maritime Services, telling Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday he expected around 20% of orders for building new dry bulk shipping vessels may be canceled in 2009; Korea's \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  sank 14.4%.\par \par \uc2 Japan's main index traded under 8000 for the first time since Oct. 29; banks were hit with \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial\uc2  tumbling 10% and \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  11% while insurer \uc2 Tokio Marine Holdings\uc2  shed 15% after reporting poor first half results and terminating its plan for a share buyback.\par \par \uc2 Miners and financials fell in Australia with BHP Billiton down 9.1% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  down 13%; \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  lost 5.9% with Commonwealth Bank 5.2%.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong property stocks were hit with that subindex down 7% and Cheung Kong down 5.9%; \uc2 HSBC\uc2  fell 4.2% on worries about the outlook for business in Europe and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index fell 1.6% after a 6.1% rally Wednesday; Singapore's Straits Times Index was down 4.1% with Malaysia's main index falling 2.0%, Indonesian shares 3.5% and Thai shares 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's NZX-50 closed with a 2.3% fall to its lowest level since June 2004, while Philippine shares ended down 3.5% decline.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar fell to Y95.15 with the euro at Y118.80; the euro was around $1.2490 while the Australian dollar slipped back to US$0.6346.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were active: The U.S. dollar rose above S$1.53 against the Singapore dollar and against the Korean won it hit its highest level since March 1998, while it reached a 2008 high against the Malaysian ringgit; against the Thai baht, it touched its best level since March 2007.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of selling U.S. dollars to support the peso, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also spotted in Indonesia, Korea and India; in Hong Kong, authorities again sold Hong Kong dollars to preserve the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 "Though valuations might look cheap for some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this is typically not the time that investors take on more risk. Given how brutal this year has proved for many investors, we sense this adage may hold particularly true this year," said analysts at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds rose with futures adding 0.49 to 139.32; credit default swaps widened with the iTraxx Japan Series 10 index at 330 basis points from 290 basis points Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Toyota CDS were at 170 basis points, from 135 basis points on Nov. 18 and 55 basis points on Oct. 2; "pretty wide, huh? Toyota used to trade almost the same level as sovereign" CDS, said a credit trader at a Western bank in Tokyo, adding "autos are just taking it on the chin - everyone's keeping an eye on what's happening with the Big Three in Detroit."\par \par \uc2 The cost of protection in Asia ex-Japan widened, too, with the five-year iTraxx investment grade index quoted at 425-470 basis points versus a 420 basis-point midspread close Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasurys nudged up in Tokyo after strong U.S. gains with the 10-year note up 7/32 at 103 14/32, yielding 3.342%; players were betting on a further Fed rate cut in December.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $735.20, up 30 cents from New York; front-month Nymex crude was down 93 cents at $52.69 a barrel on Globex - a 22-month low - after declining 77 cents in New York.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 20-11-08 0711GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081120e4bk00089\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
