\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Yen could rise to 80/dollar - Sakakibara\b0\par\par\uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2008\par 05:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 5 (Reuters) - The yen could rise to 80 per dollar as the carry trade that has depressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for several years is unwound, Eisuke Sakakibara, a former top official at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was changing hands around 99.41 per dollar at 0500 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "Because of the yen carry trade, the yen has depreciated fairly significantly over the last four or five years," said Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for spearheading \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while vice finance minister in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 "So what is happening right now is the unwinding process of this cheap yen bubble," he said.\par \par \uc2 The yen has risen 11.6 percent against the dollar so far this year as investors have unwound investments funded in yen.\par \par \uc2 "Until dollar/yen reached something like 80, I think we shouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told reporters on the sidelines of a seminar in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "It could go to 80 -- it's a very volatile market."\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara said the unwinding process could take the yen to higher levels against the euro and Australian dollar too, adding that he would not be surprised to see the euro at 110 or 100 yen by the year-end. At 0500 GMT, euro/yen <EURJPY=> was at 127.70.\par \par \uc2 When asked about the Bank of Japan's surprise rate cut last week in response to the financial crisis, a cut that took Japan's already low policy rates to 0.3 percent from 0.5 percent, Sakakibara said he would not recommend cutting rates once again to zero.\par \par \uc2 "Japan is now in recession. Cutting interest rates by 20 basis points doesn't really affect anything but it is a symbolic gesture on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I don't think they should cut it to zero. Of course they could engage in quantitative easing."\par \par \uc2 CRISIS\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be tempted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen rose to levels near 80 per dollar. But he also felt joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the major central banks was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 "Co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think, is impossible because the dollar/euro rate is quite stable.\par \par \uc2 "It's the question of yen alone getting stronger. So, unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible but coordination \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not feasible," he said.\par \par \uc2 The former Japanese finance ministry official reckoned the credit crisis that has plunged markets to new lows and driven major economies into recession was deeper and could run longer than the Asian financial crisis a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 He also told the seminar the time was right for Asian governments to set aside part of their $4 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to create an Asian monetary fund. ((Reporting by Vidya Ranganathan, editing by Neil Fullick))\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|J|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081105e4b50007o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
