\par\par \b \uc2 TAKE A LOOK-Global stocks rise, oil slides\b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2008\par 02:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  Stocks rallied around the world on Thursday on optimism that interest rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve and other central banks will ease a looming global recession, even as oil prices fell on concerns  about slowing demand.\par \par \uc2  Stocks surged after rate cuts by the Federal Reserve on Wednesday and by Taiwan and Hong Kong on Thursday. The Federal Reserve also opened four more dollar swap lines to other countries.\par \par \uc2  Japan and Germany also said they would plough billions of dollars into their economies to help cushion them against any  deep recession, while the International Monetary Fund also launched a short-term financing facility for developing countries that have a good economic track record but have seen credit markets dry up.\par \par \uc2  The monetary policy moves bolstered risk appetite as the price of safe-haven government debt and gold fell, while the U.S. dollar rallied against the euro and the yen, buoyed by demand from U.S. corporations and global fund managers seeking to square their books or rebalance their portfolios by month-end.\par \par \uc2  MSCI's benchmark world stocks index gained <.MIWD00000PUS> gained 3.69 percent, while its emerging markets equities index <.MSCIEF> rallied nearly 11 percent.\par \par \uc2  To read more, double-click on the square brackets below:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par   MARKET REPORTS:\par > GLOBAL MARKETS-Global stocks rise, oil slides            [ID:nN30596274]\par > MONEY MARKETS-Dollar rates fall, banks lean on Fed       [ID:nN30619773]\par > EMERGING MARKETS-Latam assets gain on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves [ID:nN30288123]\par > COMMODITIES