\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Pressure Off The BOJ, But For How Long?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 1,009 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2008\par 20:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Strong rallies for global stock markets have partially reversed the yen's recent gains, relieving some of the pressure on Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ascent.\par \par \uc2 But with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continuing to behave in an unseemly fashion, swinging wildly from day to day, it's the Bank of Japan's intentions that still have everyone wondering.\par \par \uc2 Risk aversion is the driving factor for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  these days, with the yen advancing every time investors seek to escape chancier positions, then retreating as risk appetite increases. The result has been extreme volatility - last Friday alone, during Asian trading, the dollar slumped to a 13-year low just above Y90 from Y98 at the start of the session.\par \par \uc2 As markets struggle with the credit crunch and the specter of global recession, the Group of Seven nations gave \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  the go-ahead to dust off the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sword with which it has threatened unruly markets in the past. So far, there's no indication the BOJ has exercised that option.\par \par \uc2 With history as a guide, the yen's current levels still look ominous enough to nudge the BOJ into taking action to at least hinder further yen appreciation.\par \par \uc2 In the past twenty years, Japan has sought to influence markets dozens of times, using every form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  known to monetary authorities. Jawboning, solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with one or more central banks have all been carried out with varying degrees of success.\par \par \uc2 But this time round, the BOJ will have to act alone, albeit with the blessing of other central banks. In a TV interview Monday, following the G7's emergency statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde said "possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " would be "purely Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Market analysts have picked up on that message. The best guess is that while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be necessary at this point, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on its own should it come in the near future.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think we'll see coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Andrew Milligan, head of global strategy at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2  in Edinburgh.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the dollar around Y90 would be "getting far too dangerous for the Japanese economy," Milligan added.\par \par \uc2 Since Friday, the yen has given up some of its gains on the back of a global stock market rally, with the dollar rising to Y98.65 late afternoon Thursday in New York. Perhaps markets on their own have settled the matter more effectively than any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     BOJ Response Still A Hot Topic \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nevertheless, the BOJ's possible response to last week's huge surge for the yen remains a hot topic for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. There's a significant chance sentiment will boomerang yet again, stocks will drop once more and the dollar will resume its slide towards Y90.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent yen movements and the G7 statement make it at least plausible that the BOJ is weighing some sort of market action. The yen has advanced so far so fast that Japanese banks as well as manufacturers are threatened. Because an overly strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hurts the competitiveness of the groups on which Japan depends for much of its economic well-being, steady gains have always been the main reason behind BOJ attempts to de-horn yen bulls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts believe that the dollar at Y95 is the break-even level for Japanese banks, while the country's exporters need the dollar above Y105 or Y106 to remain profitable. At other times, different "lines in the sand" have been suggested for the levels which would prompt BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including Y120 and Y110. The BOJ never acknowledges target levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Publicly, the BOJ and other central banks tend to defend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with references to "disorderly market conditions" or "excess volatility." Monetary authorities are also fond of describing exchange-rate trends they don't like as "extreme \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements that aren't justified by fundamentals."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the dollar/yen fluctuations aren't driven by speculative bets these days. Instead, markets have seen an enormous unwinding of positions - to a large part funded with borrowed money - as investors were forced to return to the relative safety of the U.S. and Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The yen's dash to 13-year lows last week was a by-product of the liquidity crunch, not a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis in and of itself.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks have learned not to try to stand in the way of shifts of funds by both long- and short-term investors on the scale seen reflected in the yen recently. Even a concerted G7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have been futile. Should such an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fail, central banks would lose all credibility and unstoppable panic selling could set in.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the BOJ has become skillful at fighting delaying actions. During a 15-month period that ended in March 2004, it spent an estimated Y35 trillion on covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the yen's appreciation. It was to some degree successful in that it hindered a too-rapid advance but ultimately couldn't stop the yen from moving higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Perhaps that's the strategy it may choose this time around to reinforce the message that it doesn't want its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  driven higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Flint, a news editor in the money group, has covered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past 10 years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia. He can be reached at 201 938 4408, or via email at: robert.flint@dowjones.com.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 10-30-08 1512ET ]\f28 \par \