\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: BOJ To Mull Rate Cut As Japan Readies Econ Package\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Takeuchi and Kenneth McCallum   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,115 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2008\par 08:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A possible monetary easing by the Bank of Japan this Friday underlines how Japan is pursuing all of the policy options at its disposal to prevent its economy from being pulled down by a global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 An official familiar with the BOJ's thinking said Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board would consider cutting the overnight call rate to 0.25% from 0.50% to help the export-dependent economy, which has been pummeled by tumbling share prices, a sharply stronger yen and weakening overseas demand.\par \par \uc2 The official said the board would make its decision only after watching how the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets move in the run-up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Friday meeting, and after studying recent economic data.\par \par \uc2 The fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering a policy easing at all shows how worried Japanese policy-makers have become about a sharp downturn in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Even a week ago, top BOJ officials were all but ruling out a near-term policy easing, saying interest rates were already low enough to support the economy. But this week, financial conditions deteriorated abruptly: The Nikkei 225 Stock Average fell below 7000 for the first time in 26 years Wednesday, while the yen stayed strong after touching a 13-year high of Y90.87 to the dollar last Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The typical reaction to these market moves (among BOJ officials) is to repeat 'Why?' over and over," said the official. "They still remain a little skeptical about the effectiveness of a rate cut per se. But now, the consensus is forming that all available steps must be taken to tame these panic moves in markets."\par \par \uc2 That feeling is shared by government officials. Prime Minister Taro Aso is due to unveil Thursday a new series of measures to help the economy and improve market sentiment. It will be the second package that Japan has cobbled together this year.\par \par \uc2 Among other steps, the government wants to raise a cap on possible injections of taxpayers' money into domestic banks from Y2 trillion, loosen capital adequacy requirements for banks, and enlarge tax breaks for stock investors. Kyodo News also reported Wednesday that the government and ruling coalition have agreed to around Y2 trillion in tax cuts and Y5 trillion in fiscal spending measures.\par \par \uc2 And, in an effort to short-circuit the yen's surge, Japan got its Group of Seven rich nation counterparts to agree to warn against an excessively sharp yen rise, which threatened to knock out Japan's exporters.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about the recent excessive volatility in the exchange rate of the yen and its possible adverse implications for economic and financial stability," G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors said in a statement released Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market took that to be a warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. Traders said the impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely wouldn't last long, because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to act alone and it wouldn't have enough fire power to turn around the enormous market forces pushing the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Benefits Of Rate Cut Questioned \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts question whether all these measures will actually help shield Japan from global economic and financial turmoil. But policy makers have to do something.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With the yen and stock prices being as they are, (the BOJ officials in charge of policy) would take any policy steps to put an end to 'depression-like' sentiment prevailing in markets," the official familiar with the BOJ's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market moves on Wednesday may have gladdened the BOJ: the Nikkei jumped 7.7% to close at 8211.90, while the dollar has come back up to around Y96.80. But the Nikkei is still about 46% lower than its level at the start of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said a possible BOJ rate cut this week would be just what the government wanted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A monetary easing would complement Prime Minister Aso's soon-to-be unveiled economic package. It would also make Tokyo's job easier if it enters the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen. And after seeing how much the yen fell and Japanese stock futures rose on overnight reports that the BOJ would consider a rate cut, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be afraid of a reversal - a yen rise and tanking share prices - if it doesn't loosen policy after all on Friday, Ueno said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government is getting the BOJ surrounded," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At Friday's meeting, if BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa and newly-appointed Deputy Gov. Hirohide Yamaguchi push for a rate cut, most of the policy board members will likely toe the line, said the official familiar with the BOJ's thinking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market interest rates on Wednesday implied about a 60% chance that the BOJ would go ahead with a 25 basis point cut.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the BOJ chooses to loosen policy, it would represent an end to a tightening cycle that began in March 2006, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  scrapped its "quantitative easing" policy. The move would signal that the BOJ is cooperating with global monetary easing efforts. The U.S. Federal Reserve is widely expected to cut its benchmark interest rate Wednesday, possibly to as low as 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts said a BOJ rate cut by itself likely won't do much to stimulate demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This would have limited effect unless accompanied by other actions from the BOJ", such as commitment not to tighten until certain conditions are met, said Richard Jerram, economist at Macquarie.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data released Wednesday suggested that shaky overseas economies are taking their toll on the Japanese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial output posted its biggest fall in nearly four years in the July-September period, dropping 1.2% from the previous quarter, the government said. Production is also expected to fall in October and November, according to a government survey of manufacturers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the BOJ does cut rates to 0.25% and the economy keeps worsening, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might need to consider lowering interest rates even further to 0%, or restarting quantitative easing, which involves pumping more cash into the market than banks need, to boost demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the official said that's a question for another day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's too early to think about the future. The agenda is to put an end to this panic," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi and Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com; ken.mccallum@dowjones.com [ 29-10-08 0712GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1187 4256 5008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument D