\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-TOPWRAP 3-Japan grapples with yen, crisis grips Indonesia\b0\par\par\uc2 1,113 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Japan tries to talk down yen, bans naked short selling\par \par \uc2 * Indonesia grapples with response as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumps\par \par \uc2 * Bank of England says financial system healing\par \par \uc2 * GM, Chrysler said seeking U.S. help for merger\par \par \uc2 * South Korean banks get access to Fed funding (Fixes wording in paragraph 2 to unspecified from specified) (For full crisis coverage, double click on [nCRISIS])\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Japan fought to hold back waves from the financial crisis, restricting investor bets on falling share prices and trying to talk down a rallying yen that threatens to deepen its economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets recovered some lost ground, but a steep decline in Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the threat of an unspecified government response kept markets on edge as investors scrambled for the relative safety of the dollar and yen.\par \par \uc2 Faced with a funding squeeze and an economic slump across much of the industrialised world, companies joined banks in the queue for government aid.\par \par \uc2 U.S. automakers \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler sought government cash for a merger, South Korean banks tapped a Federal Reserve funding window, Russia was in talks with China for export-backed loans for its companies and Kazakhstan pumped $5 billion into its banks. Governments around the world have agreed to inject more than $4 trillion into banks to contain the financial crisis, and Bank of England said the strong medicine appeared to be working.\par \par \uc2 "Exceptional \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by governments and central banks should help stabilise the banking system in the period ahead," the bank said in twice yearly report..\par \par \uc2 "While there are still risks in the wider financial system, the immediate response to the measures has been positive."\par \par \uc2 The Japanese banking system, which largely escaped the fallout from U.S. mortgage defaults last year, began to look shaky this week as the stock market crashed, losing a third of its value in October.\par \par \uc2 Japanese banks, which mostly avoided instruments linked to U.S. mortgages, had invested in the stock market, and the three largest lenders are looking to replenish capital lost on the bourse.\par \par \uc2 CRITICAL\par \par \uc2 The government banned naked short selling with immediate effect on Tuesday, bringing the move forward by one week. Naked short selling allows traders to effectively sell stocks they don't already own and without borrowing them first in the hope that they will profit by buying stocks back at a lower price.\par \par \uc2 "Similar restrictions have already been put in place in the United States and Europe but Japan has lagged behind," Nakagawa said. "I found a lag of a few days is critical for the Tokyo stock market."\par \par \uc2 In Indonesia, President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono said he could unleash a new policy response after the rupiah tumbled almost 9 percent to a 7-\u189\'00\'BD year low and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to slow the slide. He gave no details.\par \par \uc2 The high-yielding rupiah <IDR=> had held relatively stable earlier this year, underpinned by foreign demand for high-yielding government bonds. But it has lost almost 14 percent this week as investors dumped riskier emerging-market assets.\par \par \uc2 The yen is being lifted by the same tide of capital, having been the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors borrowed to finance bets on high yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 The surging yen has compounded fears among exporters as their key markets lurch toward recession.\par \par \uc2 The yen has leapt about 20 percent on a trade-weighted basis <.IBOXXFXJPY> this month alone, as investors have unwound risky trades and rushed to the relative safety of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise in the past week is astonishing, but it does not reflect Japan's economic fundamentals," Japanese Economics Minister Kaoru Yosano told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors singled out the yen on Monday in a rare statement warning that its rally threatened stability, raising the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, the yen barely paused for breath after the statement as investors bet \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's G7 partners had little appetite to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the midst of the financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 With the yen trading at around 94 per dollar, Carlos Ghosn, chief executive of \uc2 Nissan Motor Co\uc2  and Renault , said it would be difficult for Nissan to compete, especially with U.S. car sales plunging 26 percent in September from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody reasonable is going to tell you that next year we're going to be out (of this crisis)," he said.\par \par \uc2 SURVIVAL\par \par \uc2 U.S. automakers are faring much worse than their Japanese rivals and two of America's "Big Three" are planning a merger to survive the crisis.\par \par \uc2 General Motors Corp\uc2  and \uc2 Chrysler LLC\uc2 's owner, \uc2 Cerberus Capital Management\uc2  asked the U.S. government for a $10 billion rescue package to support the merger, sources familiar with the talks said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. government cobbled together a $700 billion plan to bailout Wall Street last month, after mortgage defaults and credit writedowns wrecked lenders and insurers.\par \par \uc2 More banks lined up for cash on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Financial companies including \uc2 Comerica Inc\uc2  , \uc2 SunTrust Banks Inc\uc2  and \uc2 State Street Corp\uc2  agreed to sell stakes to the U.S. Treasury Department in exchange for cash infusions, part of the rescue plan approved by Congress earlier this month. [ID:nN27303243]\par \par \uc2 Two South Korea banks joined the queue for U.S. government dollars on Tuesday as the country grappled with an acute dollar funding squeeze and a crisis of investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 State-owned \uc2 Korea Development Bank\uc2  (KDB) [KDB.UL] said the U.S. Federal Reserve had agreed to buy up to $830 million in bonds for dollar funds, the first time a South Korean bank has been allowed to do so. \uc2 Kookmin Bank\uc2  [060000.KS] also said it was lining up to tap the same Fed facility.\par \par \uc2 The Fed had signalled last week its funding of short-dated securities purchases to free up frozen lending markets could include a wider range of issuers than a previously selected 50 financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 South Korea has felt the brunt of the crisis in Asia and its banks, with their large short-term foreign borrowing, have looked vulnerable to the credit crunch.\par \par \uc2 In another cross-border funding deal, Russia and China are preparing to sign a long-term oil supply agreement on Tuesday and Beijing is in talks to lend Russian companies $20 billion to $25 billion in export-backed loans, industry sources said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 And the government of Kazakhstan offered capital injections to the four largest local banks for a total of up to $5 billion. (Writing by Alan Raybould; Editing by Dayan Candappa)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 3, CORRECTED)|LANGEN|J|IF|Z|SI|PHD|AUF|ND|UKI|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081028e4as000ki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley10