\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan tries to talk down yen as economy sags\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto   \par \uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 02:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For more stories on the financial crisis click  (Adds details)\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Japan tried to push back the waves of the financial crisis on Tuesday, joining the United States and Europe in restricting investor bets on falling share prices and attempting to talk down a surging yen, an added worry for an economy already teetering on the brink of recession.\par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Kaoru Yosano played down the need to cut interest rates, arguing that reducing a benchmark rate that is already low at just 0.5 percent would do little for either the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the world's second-biggest economy.\par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan reviews rates on Friday.\par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei average dropped more than 2 percent to a 26-year low below 7,000 points, but that proved a trigger for buyers who prompted a dramatic turnaround in the index to leave the closing level up more than 6 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2  Reflecting growing concern over Japan's volatile markets, Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa said the government would ban so-called naked stock short-selling from Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  This is a tactic where traders effectively sell stocks they don't yet own and without borrowing them first, in the hope of profiting by buying them back at a lower price.\par \par \uc2  "Similar restrictions have already been put in place in the United States and Europe but Japan has lagged behind," Nakagawa said. "A lag of a few days is critical for the Tokyo stock market."\par \par \uc2  Investors are still allowed to sell short as long as they have borrowed stock against the position.\par \par \uc2  YEN LEAP\par \par \uc2  The yen has leapt about 20 percent on a trade-weighted basis this month alone as investors unwound carry trades, a phenomenon of Japan's low interest rates, in which they borrowed yen to finance investments in higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2  "The yen's rise in the past week is astonishing, but it does not reflect Japan's economic fundamentals. It is caused by unwinding of yen carry trades and selling by investment funds," Yosano told a news conference after a cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2  The Group of Seven rich nations warned on Monday that the yen's wild swings threatened financial stability, stirring speculation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally.\par \par \uc2  "The G7 would take the same stance if any other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed such excessive moves," Yosano said of the rare statement.\par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped back, as caution grew about the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as some market players took profits on last week's surge. At 0740 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was changing hands at 94.70 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders remained nervous at the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nakagawa would not be drawn on the issue.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's G7 partners would have little appetite for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid the financial crisis. Instead, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to go it alone but was also unlikely to step into the market unless the yen rises beyond 90 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  Major Japanese exporters have been hit by the surge in the yen, which reduces the value of their overseas sales.\par \par \uc2 Honda Motor Co\uc2 , Japan's No.2 automaker, cut its annual operating profit forecast by 13 percent on Tuesday, due partly to the yen's rise, while \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  halved its operating profit forecast last week.\par \par \uc2  NO RATE CUT EXPECTED\par \par \uc2  Tightening short-selling rules was part of steps outlined by the government to ease the strain on banks, which have heavy exposure to domestic equities.\par \par \uc2  They have little exposure to the risky credit instruments that crippled Wall Street, but investors fear lenders' extensive shareholdings and rising bad-loan costs will shred their profits.\par \par \uc2  Government efforts to stabilise markets have provided little respite. The Nikkei rose on Tuesday but the benchmark has lost half its value so far this year, much bigger than the Dow Jones industrial average, which is down less than 40 percent.\par \par \uc2  Major central banks earlier this month cut interest rates in an unprecedented co-ordinated move to contain the damage from a credit rout set off by the U.S. subprime meltdown 15 months ago.\par \par \uc2  But the Bank of Japan opted out, saying rates in Japan were already very low. Economics minister Yosano suggested the view had not changed.\par \par \uc2  "It may be an important proof of international cooperation if Japan lowers rates when other central banks cut them," he said.\par \par \uc2  "But when you think about the real economy, I don't think a rate cut to 0.25 percent from 0.5 percent would suddenly reverse the flow of money or boost corporate appetite for capital spending."\par \par \uc2  The BOJ is widely expected to keep its key policy rate unchanged at a review on Friday and revise down its economic forecasts in a semi-annual economic and price outlook report.\par \par \uc2  Money market futures are pricing in about a 40 percent chance of a rate cut by the end of this year, up from 30 percent seen in previous day.  (Additional reporting by Yasuhiko Seki and Chang-Ran Kim; Editing by Neil Fullick)   \par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/JAPAN (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081028e4as0002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
