\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr back from brink as Asian shares bounce\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 22:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie dlr up from lows as Tokyo shares bounce <.TOPX>\par \par \uc2 * RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for third session, market wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  * Bonds ease from highs as Asian stocks eke out gains\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar clambered up from deep lows on Tuesday as a bounce in Asian shares and a third session of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped hold the line, at least for a moment.\par \par \uc2 The embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged up to $0.6125 <AUD=>, having touched a five-and-a-half year low at $0.6007 early in the day even as the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) stood in the market as a lonely buyer.\par \par \uc2 Speculation the Japanese might use their huge firepower to slow the yen's ascent helped put a floor under the Aussie late in the day, while a bounce in battered Tokyo stocks <.TOPX> stemmed the flight to safety. "There's a good case for the Japanese to act to break this negative feedback dynamic, and that's with or without the G7," said Tony Morriss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ. The Group of Seven industrialised countries on Monday publicly warned against extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, though it stopped short of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the RBA only has a modest A$36 billion ($22 billion) in reserves, the Japanese could effectively print as much yen as was needed to break the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, making the threat of combined \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a powerful one. "In any case, Aussie/yen is looking cheap enough at these levels to be attractive to long-term investors -- it's just fallen so far," added Morriss.\par \par \uc2 The local dollar had crept up to 57.41 yen <AUDJPY=R> late on Tuesday, having earlier dropped under 56.00 yen and close to last week's all-time trough around 55.15.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was still down over 40 percent since July as investors unwound a five-year long boom in carry trades -- borrowing yen at low rates to buy everything from stocks, to commodities and high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The afternoon rally in Asian shares slightly eased the recent extreme levels of risk aversion and prompted some profit-taking in bonds. Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> slid 0.120 points to 95.600, while the 10-year futures contract <YTCc1> shed 0.180 points to 94.740.\par \par \uc2 Australian bill futures <0#YBA:> were supported by expectations the RBA will cut official interest rates again at its monthly policy meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 Investors are pricing in an easing of at least 50 basis points in the 6.0 percent cash rate. They also expect cuts in the U.S., UK and euro zone this week and next.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  survey of business conditions reminded the market there were plenty of domestic reasons for further easing. The survey's main index of conditions fell a steep 10 points to -4 in the third quarter, the lowest reading \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par since mid-2001 [nSYD73244]. ----------------(Snapshot at\par 4:05p.m./0505 GMT)----------------- FUTURES                    \par            CASH YIELD 90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (DEC)\par 95.250(-0.030)  <AU3MBB=RR>  5.82 (5.80) 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1>\par (DEC) 95.600(-0.120)   <AU3YT=RR>  4.39 (4.23) 10-YR BOND\par <YTCc1> (DEC) 94.740(-0.180)   <AU10YT=RR> 5.25 (5.04) AUD/USD\par <AUD=> 0.6125 (0.611