\par\par \b \uc2 TOPWRAP 6-Rate cuts seen ahead, G7 frets about yen\b0\par\par\uc2 977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * G7 says surging yen is threat to stability\par \par \uc2 * UK's Brown hints at global rate cuts\par \par \uc2 * South Korea slashes rates 75 basis points\par \par \uc2 * Emerging stocks at 4 year lows, Wall St seen lower\par \par \uc2 (For full crisis coverage, double click on)\par \par \uc2 By Jeremy Gaunt\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven warned on Monday the surging yen posed a threat to financial and economic stability, the latest coordinated effort by the world's richest nations to curb the worst financial crisis in 80 years.\par \par \uc2 Signs the crisis was continuing to spread were evident across the world with South Korea slashing interest rates, Australia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Gulf Arab oil producers urging quicker monetary union, and the International Monetary Fund bailing out Hungary and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. Federal Reserve almost certain to slash interest rates later in the week, British Prime Minister Gordon Brown hinted that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action may be more widespread.\par \par \uc2 "Now inflation is actually coming down over the next few months and that will mean that it gives scope to all the monetary authorities, including the Bank of England, round the world to make a decision about interest rates," he told the BBC.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets continued to react sharply to the twin perils of financial crisis and global recession.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's main world stock index was down more than 3 percent, closing in on a 50 percent loss for the year to date. Its emerging market counterpart was down more tha 3.5 percent having earlier hit a 4-year lows.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 shed 4 percent and U.S. futures pointed to a poor start on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Volatility has surged across financial markets as investors are forced to sell assets which they bought with borrowed money to repay creditors or cover margin calls they must make as asset prices fall and credit limits are breached.\par \par \uc2 This deleveraging is crucial to restoring the long-term stability and health of the financial system, but it is the speed and severity with which it is happening that is fuelling investor panic and unnerving policymakers.\par \par \uc2 It has most recently hit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, described by David Shairp, global strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Asset Management as "the new apex of the crisis".\par \par \uc2 A brief G7 statement focused on the yen, fanning speculation of the first Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in four years.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about the recent excessive volatility in the exchange rate of the yen and its possible adverse implications for economic and financial stability," the group, comprising the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada, said.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rapid 12 percent ascent against the dollar has threatened Japanese exports as the world's second-largest economy lurches towards recession.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, however, is rising against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except for the yen, so there was scepticism about whether any coordinated action on the economy would be forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up more than 1 percent against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY MOVES\par \par \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2 , Japan's biggest bank, said it would raise up to $10.6 billion by issuing new shares, the latest sign that diving stocks are likely to shred profits at Japanese lenders.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Taro Aso said after an emergency meeting the government would expand a scheme that allows banks access to public funds and tighten rules on short-selling shares.\par \par \uc2 South Korea resorted to a record interest rate cut and Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as policymakers grappled with the 15-month-old crisis that has shattered investor confidence, and threatens a deep recession.\par \par \uc2 "Today's bigger-than-usual rate cut is aimed at helping prevent a sharp slowdown in domestic demand and the overall economy," Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seong-tae during his news conference after a 75 basis point cut to 4.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 Developing nations have also been turning to the International Monetary Fund for help to stave off the worst global financial crisis since the Great Depression in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said it had reached an agreement with Hungary to get a "substantial financing package" in the next few days that will include funding by the European Union and some individual European governments.\par \par \uc2 It agreed a $16.5 billion loan for Ukraine on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets have been particularly hard hit.\par \par \uc2 Once high-flying Chinese stocks closed at a 25-month low, with some analysts talking of panic selling.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zhou Xiaochuan warned of danger ahead for the emerging market powerhouse.\par \par \uc2 "It is necessary to strengthen our awareness of the dangers, proactively cope with the challenges, and do a solid job of preparing to face potential difficulties," he said.\par \par \uc2 HELPING HANDS\par \par \uc2 The crisis was taking a toll right across the world.\par \par \uc2 Gulf Arab oil producers said at an emergency meeting on Saturday they had already taken adequate steps to deal with the impact of the global financial crisis but that turmoil on world markets had given new urgency to their plan for monetary union.\par \par \uc2 Financial turmoil forced Sweden's Swedbank to raise more than $1.5 billion in new capital while the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected liquidity into smaller rival Carnegie.\par \par \uc2 Smaller, regional U.S. banks also started seeking help from the U.S. government.\par \par \uc2 Ohio-based KeyCorp said it would add $2.5 billion in capital from the U.S. Treasury and \uc2 Huntington Bancshares\uc2 , from the same state, said it had received preliminary approval to participate in the capital purchase programme.\par \par \uc2 They followed a $3.4 billion cash injection request from \uc2 Fifth Third Bancorp\uc2 , also based in Ohio. (Editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 6)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081030e4ar001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
