\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: In Emergency Statement, G7 Warns Against Yen Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Takeuchi and Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 07:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Finance chiefs from the Group of Seven leading nations Monday issued an emergency statement warning investors against pushing up the yen too much, partly in response to Japan's pleas for help.\par \par \uc2 In the three-sentence statement,the G7 finance leaders singled out the yen in a statement for the first time since January 2000.\par \par \uc2 The communique, which was released right after an ad-hoc news conference by Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa, suggests that not only Japan but also the U.S. and Europe now feel the yen's broad advances have gone too far.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped to as high as Y90.87 to the dollar Friday, its strongest level in 13 years. That sharp strengthening in the yen threatens to batter Japanese exports at a time when the domestic economy is going through another weak spell.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about the recent excessive volatility in the exchange rate of the yen and its possible adverse implications for economic and financial stability," the statement by the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the G7 said. "We continue to monitor markets closely, and cooperate as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders showed only a limited reaction to the statement as they preferred to study its meaning before trading on it.\par \par \uc2 "Some players had speculated before the market opened that governments may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as today, so the statement disappointed us because it suggests that authorities are still a few steps away from actually selling the yen," said Hiroshi Maeba, senior dealer at Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 "Under these unusual market conditions, we know that authorities are concerned about the yen's rise because everybody is concerned about it. What we need is an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, after the dollar rose slightly against the yen on the G7 statement, it dropped nearly a yen later, pushed down by a sharp fall in Japanese share prices. It's now trading around Y93.35.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Warning Has Presaged Action In Past \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, in the past when the G7 highlighted the yen's strength specifically but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued appreciating, that's led to market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , either by a few main central banks or by Japan by itself.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For example, in September 1999, after the G7 warned about the yen's strength, Japan's Finance Ministry and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  repeatedly sold the yen against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakagawa said the group has acted on Japan's calls for a communique, suggesting the country has a broad backing from its G7 partners: the U.S., Germany, France, the U.K., Canada and Italy. The Japanese finance minister himself threatened to "take steps" if needed in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - the words that were often used in the past to signal that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was coming.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stayed out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since March 2004, when it ended a massive yen-selling campaign to prevent a strong domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from undermining the nation's then fragile economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of both advanced and developing nations have recently become the most volatile in many years as the U.S.-led global financial crisis intensifies, prompting some countries to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. On Monday, Australia bought its beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to shore it up, while Indonesia and the Philippines did the same for their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday it bought HK$7.75 billion worth of U.S. dollars to inject liquidity into the local banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is facing different problems from those counterparts: its trouble is with a soaring domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that could hurt exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's rise has come as investors race to pull out of high-risk investments - such as oil or stocks in emerging countries bought with borrowed yen - as they grew more worried about the prospect of a deeper, prolonged global economic recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both the U.S. and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regained some ground versus the yen from late Friday through early Monday. But the market's bullish sentiment toward the yen was little changed, and the G7's latest words did little to hoist Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Because the G7 statement came completely out of the blue, they are little confused about it," said Yuji Saito, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "We need a bit more time to understand the context and background" to gauge the possibility of joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi and Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com, takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com (Takashi Mochizuki contribued to this story)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected Oct. 27, 2008 4:56 ET (0856 GMT) [ 27-10-08 0626GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7 finance leaders singled out the yen in a statement for the first time since January 2000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ("=UPDATE: In Emergency Statement, G7 Warns Against Yen Rises," which ran at 0626 GMT, misstated when a G7 statement singled out the yen.) [ 27-10-08 0854GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081027e4ar000e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
