\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA UPDATE: Korea Cuts Key Rate,Japan Watching  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Mkt\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 04:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds G7 statement on concerns about the yen)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Evan Ramstad and Yuka Hayashi \par    OF THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its key interest rate by three-quarters of a point early Monday, its biggest one-day move ever, in a sign that the global financial crisis is now reshaping Asian economies far removed from the original source of trouble in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Tokyo, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by suggesting the government is willing to counter the strengthening yen.\par \par \uc2 And in Sydney, officials acknowledged the government bought the Australian dollar on Friday to shore up its sagging value, the first time it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2007.\par \par \uc2 The actions led to mixed results in Asian stock markets at mid-morning. South Korea's broad-market Kospi index was up about 2%. Japan's Nikkei 225 Stock Average, which had fallen briefly below its lowest level since bursting of Japan's asset bubble in the late 1980s, was up 0.7%. Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index was down 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea's decision - which lowered the rate to 4.25% from 5% - was the second rate cut this month as it tries to engineer a gradual rather than abrupt slowdown in the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 The bank on Oct. 9 cut the rate by one-quarter point to 5%, but few South Korean banks followed because of the need to attract deposits in the face of the global liquidity crunch.\par \par \uc2 South Korean government officials are worried that small and midsize businesses, which rely heavily on bank loans, are being hurt by high interest rates and will be forced to cut jobs without access to bank finance.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the deepest cut on record to the main interest rate, the BOK also slashed its credit loan rate to commercial banks by three-quarters of a point to 2.5%. That special, low-interest rate is now sharply lower than the main rate.\par \par \uc2 The new cuts underscore the role sudden change of policy focus at the BOK caused by the unfolding worldwide financial crisis, which has tightened the availability of money. Only two months ago, the BOK had raised the key interest rate because of inflation fears.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, fears that the sharply rising yen would hurt the exporters on which the economy depends heavily spurred Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa to declare there was "excessive volatility" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and that the government was watching the market "with great interest."\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry also unveiled a joint statement from the Group of Seven industrial countries, saying that the nations are concerned about the excessive volatility in the yen's rate, adding that the group will "monitor markets closely and cooperate as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 After falling below 91 yen on Friday, the dollar was trading around 94.30 yen in Monday morning trading in Tokyo. Japan is expected to announce market-stabilization measures.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Evan Ramstad and Yuka Hayashi, Wall Street Journal Asia \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-10-08 0358GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081027e4ar0008v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
