\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-Milestones in the yen's history\b0\par\par\uc2 1,124 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 08:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven warned on Monday the yen's wild swings threatened financial stability, fanning speculation central banks may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  driven by a Japanese exodus from emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> was the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mentioned in a brief G7 statement as it rallied to 13-year high against the dollar, threatening Japanese exports as the world's second-largest economy tumbles toward recession amid the worst global financial crisis in 80 years.\par \par \uc2 Here are some milestones in the yen's history:\par \par \uc2 1871 - The yen became Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of the Meiji Restoration, which marked the start of Japan's modernisation and opening to the rest of the world. Japan adopted the gold standard.\par \par \uc2 1949 - After World War Two the dollar's fixed rate is set at 360 yen via the Bretton Woods system, partly to help stabilise prices in the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 1959 - The dollar/yen exchange rate is liberalised and the margin of fluctuation is set at 0.5 percent on either side of its dollar parity.\par \par \uc2 1963 - The margin of fluctuation is widened to 0.75 percent. 1971 - United States abandons gold standard, bringing an end to the Bretton Woods system of fixed exchange rates and forcing a realignment of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dec. 1971 - Under the Smithsonian Agreement, the dollar/yen exchange rate is set at 308 yen and is allowed to fluctuate in a wider band between 301.07 yen and 314.93 yen.\par \par \uc2 1973 - Japanese monetary authorities decide to let the yen float freely against the dollar, and the yen appreciates as far as 263 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 1978 - The yen pushes through 200 to the dollar for the first time, strengthening as far as 177.\par \par \uc2 1980 to 1985 - The yen's appreciation halts and partially reverses despite Japan's big trade surpluses. Higher interest rates in the United States prompt Japanese investors to put money in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 1985 - The Group of Five industrial nations, the predecessor to the G7, sign the Plaza Accord in which they agree the dollar is overvalued and to weaken it. The yen climbs from its pre-accord level of around 240 to 211 in October and 200 in November, a 20 percent rise in just a few months.\par \par \uc2 1986 - The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls further to around 190 yen in January, 167 yen in April and 153 yen in August.\par \par \uc2 1987 - In February, six of the G7 nations sign the Louvre Accord, which aims to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and halt the dollar's broad decline. The dollar still falls from near 153 to 137 in April and 120.80 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 1988 - On Jan. 4, the dollar falls to a post-war low of 120.45 yen in Tokyo trade, a level that holds as the low for more than five years. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen that day on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aug. 17, 1993 - The dollar declines to a new post-war low of 100.40 yen in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 June 21, 1994 - The dollar falls through the key 100 yen level and touches a record postwar low of 99.85 yen in New York trade before finishing at 100.30 yen.\par \par \uc2 April 19, 1995 - The dollar hits a record post-war low at 79.75 yen after U.S.-Japanese trade frictions spark heavy selling. By the end of the year it is near 103.40.\par \par \uc2 June 17, 1998 - As the dollar shoots above 144 yen, U.S. authorities join the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to buy yen, spending $833 million. By August the dollar rises to near 148 yen, partly due to yen carry trades in which investors borrow yen funds at Japan's near zero interest rates to buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1998 - After the global financial market strains from the near collapse of hedge fund \uc2 Long-Term Capital Management\uc2 , carry trades are unwound quickly. In one week alone in October, the dollar tumbles from near 136 yen to a low around 111.50 yen. 1999 - The yen strengthens further despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reaching 102 in November.\par \par \uc2 2001 - Following the Sept 11 attacks, \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 2003 - The MOF begins massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar, partly to shield Japanese exporters as the economy remains stuck in its post-bubble slump and deflation. The MOF spends 20.4 trillion yen ($200 billion) over the year, nearly all of it to buy dollars and sell yen.\par \par \uc2 2004 - The MOF spends 14.8 trillion yen ($145 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of the year, including 1.67 trillion yen buying dollars on Jan. 9 alone. But the MOF ceases \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March and has never since resumed.\par \par \uc2 2005 - The yen reaches a high of 101.67 yen in January but then starts to fall, hitting 121.40 in December. Yen carry trades and Japanese investors shifting funds into foreign assets drive the slide.\par \par \uc2 June 2007 - The dollar hits a 4-1/2-year high of 124.14 yen. July 2007 - The yen's broad depreciation takes it to a 22-year low on a real effective exchange rate basis. Since January 2005 the yen has lost 25 percent of its value on a REER basis.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 2007 - Strains in financial markets from the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis spark an unwind of yen carry trades.\par \par \uc2 The dollar falls from near 120 yen to 111.60 yen. The high-yielding Australian and New Zealand dollars tumble nearly 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 March 13, 2008 - The yen hits an 12-year high of 99.77.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 24, 2008 - Yen hits 13-year high of 90.87 versus the dollar, while setting an all-time high against the Australian dollar of 55.11, with the Aussie losing almost a third of its value in just a month on a massive unwind of carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 27, 2008 - The yen's surge to 13-year highs prompts the G7 to issue statement to single out the yen in warning on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.\par \par \uc2 The yen has surged nearly 20 percent so far in October on a trade weighted basis, more than twice as big as any month going back to 1970, including the carry trade collapse in October 1998 and the Plaza Accord to weaken the dollar in 1985.\par \par \uc2 Sources: Reuters, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , Bank of England (Writing by Mathew Veedon and Eric Burroughs; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 YEN/ (CHRONOLOGY)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|J|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081027e4ar000n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
