\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-G7 warns on yen, markets shrug off  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  risk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,125 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 05:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds France's Lagarde)\par \par \uc2 * G7 says prepared to act on excessive yen swings, if needed\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk gathers steam, yen holds gains\par \par \uc2 * Australia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for 2nd day\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven rich nations tried to rein in the surging yen, saying its wild swings threatened stability, but failed to convince investors they were ready to jump into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the midst of a financial storm.\par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors said they were prepared to act, if necessary, as the yen rocketed toward a 13-year high against the dollar, driven by an exodus of Japanese cash from emerging markets and other high-yielding investments.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about the recent excessive volatility in the exchange rate of the yen and its possible adverse implications for economic and financial stability," the G7 said in a brief statement.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to monitor markets closely, and cooperate as appropriate," the group, comprising the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada, said.\par \par \uc2 The yen barely paused for breath after the statement as investors bet Japan's G7 partners had little appetite for a fight with markets as they grappled with worst financial turmoil in 80 years.\par \par \uc2 This view gained ground after French Economy Minister Christine Lagarde told Bloomberg News the G7 was not planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The yen has over the past 48 hours seen brutal trading that reflects a great volatility that's linked to current market moves," she was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 "We wished to support this possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japanese authorities knowing this would be about a purely Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said of the statement issued by the G7.\par \par \uc2 Asked specifically if the G7 would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen, Lagarde said "no".\par \par \uc2 PANICKY MOVES\par \par \uc2 Some analysts viewed the G7 statement as a hint at possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially after the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market for a second day to support its dollar near record lows against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "Given the panicky and irrational movements of the yen of late, the Japanese authorities may conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  independently," said Kazuyuki Kato, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Mizuho Trust & Banking in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "Such action may be taken if the dollar falls below the 90 yen level. But given the fact that the dollar is rising against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except for the yen, Japan is not likely to be able to win support for coordinated action."\par \par \uc2 But a former \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  official had a word of caution.\par \par \uc2 "The G7 statement showed the group's strong willingness to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, particularly to curb excessive volatility in yen moves, with an eye on a possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Eiji Hirano told Reuters. Hirano, now a senior executive with \uc2 Toyota Financial Services Corp\uc2 ., was assistant governor in charge of G7 talks until mid-2006.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2004.\par \par \uc2 But the world's second-largest economy, which boasts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves just shy of $1 trillion, has repeatedly taken on markets in the past decades, mostly to prevent the yen's gains from hurting its export champions, such as Toyota or \uc2 Sony\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During the last episode, Japan bought $145 billion in the first quarter of 2004 to stem the yen's rise, piling on top of $200 billion spent in the previous year.\par \par \uc2 NEAR HIGHS\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the yen traded near an all-time high against the Australian dollar and near a 13-year peak against the U.S. dollar, as Tokyo shares slumped to a 26-year low, spurring a renewed wave of selling of higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It traded at around 93 to the dollar <JPY=>.\par \par \uc2 Fears that the financial crisis set off by a global credit crunch will drive the world economy into a deep and damaging recession, pummelled stock markets worldwide and wreaked havoc in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The flight of shell-shocked investors from emerging markets and massive unwinding of investments in higher-yielding but riskier assets, has propelled the yen -- a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice for such deals -- to multi-year highs.\par \par \uc2 The exodus forced several nations, particularly those that have relied on foreign capital inflows to finance investment and imports, to seek help from the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 The IMF is due to announce a "substantial financing package" this week for Hungary, one of the countries that drew Japanese investors looking for returns that topped interest rates of around 0.5 percent offered at home.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar became one of the latest casualties of the unwinding of risky trades, slumping to an all-time low against the yen and triggering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BURST BUBBLE\par \par \uc2 The yen climbed nearly 19 percent so far this year against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which itself has gained substantially against many emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro, despite the grim outlook for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained 20 percent in trade-weighted terms this month alone, threatening to further undercut the Japanese economy with its exporters already smarting from a sharp downturn in the United States and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Central banks around the world repeatedly pumped billions of dollars into money markets and earlier month several of them cut interest rates in an unprecedented co-ordinated move to contain the damage from a credit rout set off by the U.S. subprime market meltdown 15 months ago.\par \par \uc2 But whereas countries such as South Korea have also frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shield their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the global upheaval, the world's top economic powers have so far refrained from taking on global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the massive de-leveraging of world financial markets and rebalancing of investment portfolios mean that efforts to stem the yen's climb may be doomed to fail.\par \par \uc2 "As the bubble is bursting everywhere from the housing market and the derivatives market to emerging markets, investors are becoming more risk-averse and pouring more money into the yen, which is considered to be the safest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ryohei Muramatsu, manager of group treasury Asia at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "Launching \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  independently is like a drop in the bucket, and like fighting against the whole world." (For a Chronology on yen, please double-click [ID:nSP231281]) (For more on the financial crisis, double-click [nCRISIS]) (Additional reporting by Eric Burroughs; Writing by Tomasz Janowski, Editing by Dayan Candappa, Swaha Pattanaik)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/G7 (WRAPUP 3)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|NAT|UKI|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|J   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081027e4ar00083\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
