\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Central banks need to calm disorderly  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Dolan and Veronica Brown   \par \uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 13:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The world's major central banks urgently need to calm wild swings in major exchange rates, the latest manifestation of a deepening global financial crisis and one which has sent the U.S. dollar and Japanese yen soaring against European and emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, which has seen moves of a staggering 10 percent on some major rates on Friday alone, could see the Group of Seven or 20 top central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon to stabilise world markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen soared on Friday against the dollar and euro and one-month implied volatility rates on the dollar/yen exchange rate surged more than 20 percentage points to almost 45 percent, prompting Japanese Vice Finance Minister Kazuyuki Sugimoto to warn against such rapid and excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2  As global investors grow fearful of a world recession and intense financial stress, they are cutting cross-border investments everywhere and repatriating money. Japan's huge external surpluses buoy its yen in that environment.\par \par \uc2  What is more, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates everywhere are expected to fall sharply soon to offset the world slowdown and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported by relatively high nominal interest rates now are suffering disproportionately from the speculation.\par \par \uc2  ING's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Chris Turner said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility over the last two months had, to a large extent, been a symptom, not a cause, of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2  But he added that this week's spike in volatility now seemed to be exacerbating global asset market declines.\par \par \uc2  "G7 central banks may need to exercise their mandate of ensuring orderly \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Turner said, adding it could come as soon as today.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ROILING ASSETS   Analysts pointed out the 6 percent collapse in the dollar/yen exchange rate towards 90 per dollar on Friday contributed to a near 10 percent fall in the Nikkei - which itself has set the tone for sharp losses on European bourses and indications of a Wall St slide too.\par \par \uc2  "The scale and pace of movements in major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates this week have been sufficiently disorderly to justify immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities," said Joe Prendergast, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in Zurich.\par \par \uc2  But Prendergast stressed the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also go well beyond the desire for calmer trading and that central banks may need to cap further sharp gains in the dollar that would compound banking and economic stress.\par \par \uc2  "The most overwhelming immediate reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provide \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity for the cross-border deleveraging and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -matching process is to stem the fear and uncertainty that this scale of seemingly counterintuitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements creates," he said.\par \par \uc2  But he added that the balance sheets of banks and financial institutions in Europe and around the world were heavily short of dollars due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mismatches created by the huge writedowns of distressed U.S. dollar mortgage assets.\par \par \uc2  The rising dollar exchange rate exaggerates those losses.\par \par \uc2  "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar gains may escalate, irrespective of fundamental considerations, compounding global financial and economic dislocations," he added.\par \par \uc2  WITHERING MOVES\par \par \uc2  The yen extended gains against the dollar and euro on Friday, with the euro down more than 10 percent to six-year lows on the yen.\par \par \uc2  Earlier the dollar soared to new two year highs versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro, while sterling hit a six-year low.\par \par \uc2  "Its extreme risk aversion and deleveraging of risky assets ...  and we are seeing safe-haven flows into dollar and yen," said Lee Hardman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at BTM-UFJ. "A sharp decline in Asian equities have added to negative sentiment."\par \par \uc2  Turner at ING said how to execute \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a problem.\par \par \uc2  "Policy makers will always prefer strength in numbers, but in which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  take place?" he said.\par \par \uc2  "Should G7 central banks be selling dollars against Europe and Canada, or be buying dollars against yen."\par \par \uc2  Analysts said G7 central bankers may be waiting to co-ordinate some response with the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2  The IMF is hurrying to approve by early November a package that would allow certain emerging market economies exchange local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars to ease short-term credit strains, officials familiar with the plans said late on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  The so-called liquidity swap facility would be available to a group of pre-selected "top tier" emerging market countries -- those that are well-run but may be having difficulties obtaining credit, the officials told Reuters\par \par \uc2  Leaders from the Group of 20 top economies, including G7 and leading emerging economy governments, are scheduled to meet in Washington on November 15 to discuss a response to the global economic and financial crisis.  (Additional reporting by Tamawa Kadoya and Naomi Tajitsu)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081024e4ao001ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
