\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Fin Min Says Dollar/Yen Not So Volatile\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 10:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Despite the dollar's brief tumble below Y100 earlier in the day, Japan's finance minister Wednesday characterized recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves as not so volatile, suggesting Tokyo isn't planning on propping up the greenback in the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 But Shoichi Nakagawa said he will closely watch global stock markets after the Nikkei 225 Stock Average posted its largest-ever drop in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 His comments suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be unwilling to rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the escalating U.S.-led global financial crisis, which economists say may be too broad and deep-rooted for any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have any lasting effect.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements) as that volatile," Nakagawa told reporters at the Finance Ministry.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the afternoon in Tokyo, the dollar fell below the Y100 mark for the first time since April. A strong yen could hurt Japan's already sputtering export-led economy by making Japanese products more expensive abroad while cutting the yen-value of overseas profits earned by manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 A senior Japanese Finance Ministry official also noted that it's not clear whether Group of Seven finance chiefs will discuss the need of any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their planned meeting Friday in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "For now, I'm not sure if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues will be discussed," the official told reporters when asked about the chance of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors agreeing on coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is usually conducted according to market conditions, so we don't necessarily decide on whether to do so (just) because the G7 meeting is being held."\par \par \uc2 But partly unnerved by the soaring yen, investors pushed the Nikkei 225 down 9.4% - its biggest one-day percentage drop since October 1987 - to 9203.32 during the session.\par \par \uc2 "Stocks fell quite sharply," Nakagawa said. "I think Japan's economic fundamentals are better than the U.S.'s or Europe's, but I must face up to the fact" market participants pulled Tokyo stocks lower, he said.\par \par \uc2 But the finance minister offered little indication that Japan is considering any action to shore up stock prices.\par \par \uc2 "I would like to closely watch European stock markets" as well as global share prices, he said.\par \par \uc2 Nakagawa, meanwhile, said he wanted to actively exchange opinions with his G7 counterparts on Friday about the state of financial markets. "Japan will say what it should say," he said, a comment that suggests he may indirectly urge the U.S. to mull using taxpayers' money to fix its battered banking sector just as Japan did in the past.\par \par \uc2 The G7 includes the U.S., Japan, Germany, the U.K., France, Italy and Canada.\par \par \uc2 The group "will likely work together closely to take appropriate steps" on the issue, the Japanese official said.\par \par \uc2 The intensifying financial crisis, which initially stemmed from the U.S.'s housing-market meltdown, "appears to be affecting the real economy worldwide ... and tensions in financial markets are growing, rattling equity markets," the official said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com (Tomoyuki Tachikawa contributed to this article.) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-10-08 0851GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081008e4a8000mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
