\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS: Japan PM Fukuda Brings Fiscal Hawks Into New Cabinet\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2008\par 10:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--In the first Cabinet reshuffle of his administration, Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda Friday picked economic ministers that appear more focused on cutting the nation's giant debt than in ensuring strong economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Fukuda likely hopes that bringing in new Cabinet ministers will help shore up his public approval rates, which have sunk to around 25% from 60% in the past 10 months because of the public's dissatisfaction with his handling of economic and other policies.\par \par \uc2 It's uncertain how the Japanese public will react to the new appointments. Global investors, on the other hand, might be disappointed by the personnel shakeup, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 That's because Fukuda left out his new Cabinet lawmakers that are known for backing reform measures to bolster economic and tax-income growth, they say. Despite mounting worries that Japan's economy may be entering a recession, Fukuda instead picked politicians that appear to share his own view that what's most needed now is to cut the national debt by raising taxes.\par \par \uc2 'The appointments of Bunmei Ibuki (as finance minister) and Kaoru Yosano (as economy minister) gave the impression that Fukuda decided to place more emphasis on fiscal rehabilitation and less on economic growth,' said Tomoyuki Ohta, senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute. 'This may disappoint foreign investors and accelerate the 'sell-Japan' mood in the short run.'\par \par \uc2 Yosano, a former Chief Cabinet Secretary, will replace Hiroko Ota as economy minister, serving as Fukuda's top economic adviser and helping draft new policies.\par \par \uc2 Yosano, who also worked as an economy minister a few years earlier, is widely seen as one of the leaders among ruling party lawmakers who want higher taxes to fix Japan's fiscal woes - in June, he proposed lifting the consumption tax rate to 10% from 5% by the mid-2010s. He is also a close ally of the powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is seeking a sales tax rise.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    How To Deal With Huge Debt\par   The finance minister post went to Bunmei Ibuki, formerly secretary general of the Liberal Democratic Party. The LDP is in a ruling coalition with junior coalition partner New Komeito.\par   Ibuki started his career as a finance ministry bureaucrat, and says in his web site that a market economy isn't a cure-all for problems, suggesting that he might also be sympathetic to the fiscally hawkish line.\par   And joining the Fukuda administration as land and transport minister was Sadakazu Tanigaki, a former finance minister who has been pushing hard to hike the consumption tax rate.\par   While Tanigaki's new portfolio will have little direct relation to Japan's tax policy, his presence in the Cabinet means that Fukuda will have one more supporter for his aggressive fiscal reform stance.\par   Japan's debts and borrowings are the highest among industrialized countries, now totaling over Y900 trillion, or 1.8 times the nation's annual gross domestic product. Many analysts have warned that Japan must do more to reduce the debts before they get out of hand.\par   But there's a heated debate going on among Japanese policy-makers on whether Japan's debts should be reduced by boosting taxes or encouraging faster economic growth, which could push up tax revenues. Some analysts were surprised that none of the members of the latter school of thought was represented in the new Cabinet.\par   Led by former chief cabinet secretary Hidenao Nakagawa, this group of politicians seeks to boost the economy's potential growing power through structural reforms and deregulation. They argue that higher economic growth would lead to increased corporate profits and workers' salaries, in turn resulting in bigger tax revenues without raising tax rates.\par   'Nakagawa as finance or economy minister could have projected an image that the new Fukuda cabinet will remain serious about various reforms,' said Hiromichi Shirakawa, Japan economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par   'For example, he has been consistently calling for further cuts in wasteful government spending before raising consumption taxes,' he said.\par    Status Quo On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Policy?\par   On the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  front, traders speculate that Ibuki will carry on the finance ministry's generally hands-off approach in recent years - unless a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis erupts. The ministry sets Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.\par   When the dollar tumbled below the psychologically important Y100 level around the middle of March, Ibuki as LDP secretary general said the move was 'not desirable.' A strong yen threatens Japan's export-dependent growth by making the nation's products more expensive abroad.\par   But he also conceded then that it is 'difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alone (to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to curb yen rises), given current economic conditions and our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.' His comments indicate his assessment that Japan can't fight the tide of massive global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets without cooperation from other industrial powers.\par   'Japan will probably continue its passive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stance, unless the dollar nosedives, prompting the U.S. to plea for coordinated action to defending its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,' said Hideaki Inoue, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking.\par -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par    (Takeshi Takeuchi and Tomoyuki Tachikawa contributed to this article.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to go to Dow Jones NewsPlus, a web front page of today's most important business and market news, analysis and commentary: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnewsplus.com/al?rnd=DH3lPMl3%2BwxoeLrPETJcbQ%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnewsplus.com/al?rnd=DH3lPMl3%2BwxoeLrPETJcbQ%3D%3D}}}\uc2 \f2 . You can use this link on the day this article is published and the following day. -0- Copyright (c) 2008 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020080804e484003ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
