\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Renewed US Optimism Buoys Dollar Vs Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Riva Froymovich   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2008\par 18:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--As the dollar sheds recent gains against the euro on downgraded expectations for a U.S. rate hike, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lead on the yen has been sustained.\par \par \uc2 The greenback's fresh vigor against the yen is indicative of growing confidence in the U.S. financial sector and depreciating fears of a recession, mostly stemming from the Federal Reserve's new vocal focus on inflation rather than growth concerns.\par \par \uc2 The result is renewed support for riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades, including investments in the dollar funded by the lower-yielding yen in a strategy called the carry trade.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has been able to capture these gains - and hold onto them - even though the outlook for Fed rate hikes due to inflation concerns has eased in the last two days because the buck already has a greater yield than the yen.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan voted just last week to leave its unsecured overnight call loan rate unchanged at 0.50% - the lowest level among Group of Seven nations - for the eighth consecutive meeting. The fed funds rate in the U.S. is currently 2.0%, whittled down from 5.25% since September 2007, while the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood pat at 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 But, if the Fed doesn't follow up its recent talk with action in the next few months, the dollar could fall to the same fate against the yen as it has against the euro, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 "Our rate outlook is firmly supportive of renewed yen strength as we believe too much Fed tightening is priced into the U.S. front end," said Thomas Stolper, an analyst at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 He added that a technical break below Y105.70 would be needed to confirm the turnaround.\par \par \uc2 "Indeed, we have considerable doubts over whether the market's current extent of hawkishness is justified in the US and Europe," echoed Daragh Maher, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon in London. "This would see the rate differential move back in the yen's favor."\par \par \uc2 "With the rest of Asia still performing well, the U.S. showing signs of recovery, and the euro zone defying various forecasts of impending doom, Japan's economy looks set to bounce back."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Why The Rebound From Y100? \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Back in March, at the height of U.S. banking sector tensions, the dollar broke below Y100 to its lowest values since mid-1995. Yet, within three months, the dollar has reversed course. It rose on Monday to its highest level against the yen since late February this year to Y108.59.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other than a sudden change in the market's outlook on Fed monetary policy, at first glance, there doesn't appear to be enough hard evidence supporting the buck's bump.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Nikkei is the best performing major market with 'only' a 6.27% decline year to date. Real GDP rose by 4% quarter-over-quarter annualized in first quarter and the Japanese banks appear to have been remarkably well protected from the global credit mess," noted Goldman's Stolper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Foreigners have even started to buy Japanese stocks again with the strongest inflows in April and May since last summer," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, Goldman has seen less carry trade-related outflows than before the credit crisis turned markets in the summer of 2007 and the Fed pared back the dollar's yield advantage to the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't even \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets when the dollar slipped under Y100, added Stolper. "We expected that this change in policy stance, combined with cheaper hedging costs could provide the impulse for more substantial hedging flows and push the Yen even stronger."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But, the overriding story was and has been a less volatile market, more robust U.S. data and hope that the economy will evade a recession. Meanwhile, the recent rise of inflation worries in the U.S. and beyond has mostly bypassed Japan. The carry trade strategy is most effective when volatility is low and the rate differential is sure to remain intact.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "U.S. fundamentals have changed....Now, people are talking about a rate hike from the Fed," said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "The financial crisis seemed to be over after the merger of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bear Stearns\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan Chase.\f28 \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are trying to get back into the carry trade market again," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June will be a particularly dollar buoyant month versus the yen, added Yanagihara, because it is bonus season for Japanese employees. Recipients are buying investment trusts abroad because of Japan's low interest rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They are looking for higher-yielding opportunities," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Riva Froymovich reports on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in New York.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Riva Froymovich, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 5063; riva.froymovich@dowjones.com \f28 \par