\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Ex-MOF Watanabe Hints  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Action Still Possible\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2008\par 08:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more remarks from Watanabe, background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tomoyuki Tachikawa \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 JEJU, South Korea (Dow Jones)--Japan's former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs said Sunday the possibility of the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets cannot be ruled out if the dollar falls sharply against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe - the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top bureaucrat on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy before stepping down last year - also suggested that Tokyo continues to favor a weak yen as inflation has yet to rise in Japan.\par \par \uc2 His remarks came after finance ministers from the Group of Eight leading nations Saturday made no mention of whether they would act together to bolster the U.S. dollar against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "What the U.S. is concerned about is how fast the dollar will fall down the road," Watanabe, currently a university professor in Tokyo, told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of the Asia-Europe Meeting held in Jeju, South Korea. "If the dollar falls sharply against the yen, the U.S. may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may help the move."\par \par \uc2 In recent days, U.S. officials dealing with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, such as Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson and Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke, have warned that the weakening dollar could fuel inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yen is generally considered beneficial to Japan's export-driven economy by making its products cheaper to consumers in the U.S. and other dollar-linked markets. But a weaker yen also makes surging global commodity prices even higher for Japan, which buys 60% of its food and almost all of its oil from overseas.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga has remained silent about U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which may indicate the Japanese government doesn't share Washington's anxiety over dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly, (some officials in Japan) appear to think that a strong yen is now beneficial for the country" given soaring raw material costs across the globe, Watanabe said. "But inflation is not rising in Japan," so the weakening yen is still considered to be helpful for the broader economy overall.\par \par \uc2 Japan's overall consumer price index rose 0.8% on year in April, although that in the U.S. climbed 4.2% in May.\par \par \uc2 Vice finance ministers for international affairs in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in consultation with finance ministers, usually decide whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to push the yen higher or lower.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe, appointed to the post in July 2004, was the first vice finance minister who didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the yen was floated in the early 1970s.\par \par \uc2 -By Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com [ 15-06-08 0653GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080615e46f0008n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
