\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Dollar may steal show at G8 inflation talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2008\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 12 (Reuters) - The dollar could grab the headlines at a weekend G8 meeting meant to focus on oil and commodity prices, especially if there are signs the shift in U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy could lead to coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not top the agenda for Group of Eight financial leaders in Japan. With central bankers absent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be mentioned in the communique, and officials have said the talks will focus on commodities and inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the strong correlation between dollar weakness and the surge in oil must surely feature in any such discussion.\par \par \uc2 And investors have detected a shift in Washington's view on the dollar after a flurry of comments from senior U.S. officials about the weak dollar stoking inflation and even raising the prospect of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So when finance ministers from the group of industrial powers convene in Osaka on Saturday, their comments will be scrutinised for clues on whether the United States will back its words with action and whether other governments will support \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Commodities will be a key topic of discussion, which means that the dollar, by default, will also be discussed," said Sharada Selvanathan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The dollar plays an important role in the commodities rally and thus in global inflation.\par \par \uc2 Selvanathan noted a study by the International Monetary Fund showing gold and oil prices rise by more than 1 percent for every 1 percent decline in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A Dallas Federal Reserve paper last month said the weaker dollar had contributed about a third of the $60 increase in oil prices between 2003 and 2007.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar-oil nexus is corrosive and damaging for the global economy," Stephen Jen, chief global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , said in a note to clients this week.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's influence on oil was believed to have been a reason for why the G7 said in April it was concerned about the impact of sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings on the global economy and was watching exchange rates closely -- an allusion to potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Almost eight years have passed since the last coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September 2000 when the Fed, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted to stem the euro's decline. \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then have been on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The United States has long been seen as holding a policy of "benign neglect" -- speaking of the virtues of a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even while reaping the benefits of a weaker one through steady export growth.\par \par \uc2 Then last week Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke flagged the change from Washington by linking the weaker dollar to inflation and saying that he is watching the dollar closely with the Treasury, words that are rarely used by a top Fed official.\par \par \uc2 That was followed by Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson saying he would not rule out dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that all options are open.\par \par \uc2 The new sense of alertness, especially from Fed officials, has helped the dollar recover some ground against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over the past six years, the dollar lost nearly half its value against the euro to hit record lows against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near $1.6020 in April. In the past two months, the dollar has clawed back nearly 4 percent to near $1.5440 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 Jen of \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  said investors should heed the change of U.S. tone because "the next step, whenever it takes place, is actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe the United States, and possibly countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, are now more worried that the dollar is driving the surge in oil prices to record peaks near $140.\par \par \uc2 One issue that will shape the environment for potential U.S., European and even Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar is the respective monetary policy stances of those countries.\par \par \uc2 It would be difficult for the Fed to prop up the dollar via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it is cutting interest rates or keeping them on hold at the same time the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is conducting euro-positive rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The ECB is expected to raise rates by a quarter-point to 4.25 percent as soon as next month, while comments from the Fed about discomfort with inflation and the need to act have stoked expectations the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start lifting rates in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 That brings the monetary policy of the world's top economic regions closer together, but not entirely.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed and ECB have a common interest on inflation, but monetary policy is still divided," said Koji Fukaya, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a note to clients that ECB officials are more tolerant of a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they try to contain price pressures, changing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape since the April gathering.\par \par \uc2 "It would be a misstep for investors to completely discount potential for further verbal or actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bar on market scepticism should be set higher," they said.\par \par \uc2 Selvanathan at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  option will remain on the table.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future if the dollar does not rise as is desired by the Fed, the U.S. Treasury and other central banks who have pegged their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar," she said.\par \par \uc2 G8/MARKETS (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080615e46c000vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
