\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves $996.975 bln at end-May\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2008\par 01:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 6 (Reuters) - Japan's foreign reserves stood at $996.975 billion at the end of May, down for the second straight month as higher U.S. yields cut the value of U.S. bonds in the reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday. [JPRES=ECI]\par \par \uc2 The euro's fall versus the dollar also dented the value of euro-denominated bonds in the stockpile, a ministry official added, declining to say which of the two factors contributed the most to a fall in the reserves.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time in four months that Japan's foreign reserves -- the world's second-largest after China's -- sank below $1 trillion, the official said.\par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves had been a record high of $1.016 trillion at the end of March, according to the ministry.\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> fell to $1.5559 at the end of May from $1.5622 at the end of April, while the yield on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note <US10YT=RR> rose to 4.063 percent at the end of May from 3.732 percent a month earlier, it added.\par \par \uc2 Japan does not give \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdowns for its external reserves, but past Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests most of Tokyo's foreign cash pile is in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ministry does not disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's international reserves, but most is in U.S. dollars as past \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken the form of dollar buying.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth a record 20 trillion yen ($188.8 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as the government waged a campaign to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from derailing Japan's then-fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has stayed out of the market since then.\par \par \uc2 The massive reserves have stirred a debate among Japanese lawmakers about whether Japan should follow countries like China into launching a sovereign wealth fund to invest some of the cash pile.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which manages the reserves, is against the idea, fearing a wealth fund will be too risky. JAPAN OFFICIAL RESERVE ASSETS (in billion dollars)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  END-MAY          END-APRIL         YEAR-AGO\par  TOTAL              996.975          1,003.836          911.137\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To view the full tables, go to the website of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mof.go.jp/english/e1c006.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mof.go.jp/english/e1c006.htm}}}\uc2 \f2  (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto)\f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|NAT|U|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080605e4650027z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
