\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  shuns  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  despite yen strength\b0\par\par\uc2 By Masayuki Kitano   \par \uc2 908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2008\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds graphic)\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 22 (Reuters) - If history is anything to go by, the yen's surge this year against the dollar raises the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect the country's important exports.\par \par \uc2  But analysts say there are significant factors behind the yen's rally this year that will almost certainly keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sidelined -- in contrast to 1995 when it spent close to $50 billion trying to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2  "This is not a situation where the Japanese economy would stall or corporate profits would fall sharply even if the yen rises to some extent," said Osamu Takashima, chief analyst for \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2  "There is not much reason now to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2  In 1995, the yen rose to a record high against the dollar of 79.75 when U.S.-Japan trade friction fanned speculation that Washington would tolerate a weaker dollar. The yen also reached a 25-year peak on a real trade-weighted basis that year.\par \par \uc2  This year, as the dollar slumped broadly against a range of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the U.S. housing downturn and credit crisis, the yen rose to a 13-year peak of 95.77 to the dollar. The dollar was trading around 103 yen <JPY=> on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  Unless the yen starts to rise broadly, Japanese authorities will probably stay out of the market, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  The rising yen may hurt exporters and so weigh on the stock market, especially given the importance of the U.S. market for automakers such as \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  .\par \par \uc2  But a strong yen can also benefit the economy by making dollar-denominated imports cheaper -- an important consideration since oil is at a record high above $135 a barrel and other raw materials prices are soaring.\par \par \uc2  "It is harder to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now compared to before. The hurdles have risen," said Tomoko Fujii, head of economics and strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2  In addition, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do little to resolve the causes of the dollar's weakness, most lately the impact of the credit crisis on the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2  "If, under a risk scenario, the dollar were to fall to around 90 yen to 80 yen then I think \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would remain an option," said \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's Fujii. "But if the situation were to deteriorate that far, that means there is a crisis somewhere that needs to be fixed and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be a logical way to do so."\par \par \uc2  BROADER FOCUS\par \par \uc2  The emergence of China is also another factor that argues against an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2  It would be hard to justify yen selling at a time when the Group of Seven powers, which include Japan, have been calling on China to allow greater exchange rate flexibility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, has opted to stay out of the market since selling a massive 35 trillion yen between January 2003 and March 2004, when the world's second-largest economy was mired in deflation. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ministry's behalf.\par \par \uc2  The yen's real effective exchange rate (REER) <JPYEEXR=J>, which compares the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with movements in those of its trading partners, rose to 103.6 in March 2008, the highest since May 2006.\par \par \uc2  But that was 29 percent below its level in August 1995, the last time the yen traded above this year's high against the dollar, partly reflecting the strength of Asian and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen.\par \par \uc2  The yen's REER hit 165.5 in April 1995, the highest on Bank of Japan data going back to January 1970.\par \par \uc2  "If the real exchange rate were to rise to more than around 120, then there might be some possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubhishi UFJ's Takashima.\par \par \uc2  The yen's REER moved roughly between 116 and 124 during \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 to 2004.\par \par \uc2  For a graphic of the REER, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/BOJ_REERFP.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/BOJ_REERFP.jpg}}}\par \par \uc2  Comments from Japan's top financial diplomat, Naoyuki Shinohara, also suggest that officials are putting more weight on the yen's REER than the exchange rate with the dollar.\par \par \uc2  Shinohara said in April that the yen's exchange rate with the the dollar was no longer so closely correlated with the yen's REER.\par \par \uc2  The vice finance minister for international affairs said the gap was partly due to the fact that the proportion of Japan's trade with the United States had declined, while that with other regions such as Asia had grown.\par \par \uc2  "They used to move in line with each other, but the gap between the two rates has been growing since about 1-2 years ago," he said.\par \par \uc2  It was hard to say whether the yen's rate against the dollar or the real trade weighted value was the more appropriate gauge, he said.\par \par \uc2  "We cannot fully explain the market situation by looking at the yen's relations with the dollar alone," Shinohara said.  ($1=102.96 Yen)  (Editing by Neil Fullick)  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN (ANALYSIS, UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|J|UKI|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080525e45m0004r\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx200